const bibleBooks=[{"id":0,"name":"Genesis","chapters":50,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Genesis is a name taken from the Greek, and signifies “the book of generation or production;” it is properly so called, as containing an account of the origin of all things. There is no other history so old. There is nothing in the most ancient book which exists that contradicts it; while many things recorded by the oldest heathen writers, or to be traced in the customs of different nations, confirm what is related in the book of Genesis."],"titles":["God creates heaven and earth · The creation of light","The first sabbath · Particulars about the creation","The serpent deceives Eve · Adam and Eve transgress the Divine command, and fall into sin and misery","The birth, employment, and religion of Cain and Abel · Cain murders Abel, The curse of Cain","Adam and Seth · The patriarchs from Seth to Enoch","The wickedness of the world which provoked God's wrath · Noah finds grace","Noah, and his family and the living creatures, enter the ark, and the flood begins · Noah shut in the ark","God remembers Noah, and dries up the waters · The ark rests on Ararat, Noah sends forth a raven and a dove","God blesses Noah, and grants flesh for food · Blood, and murder forbidden","The sons of Noah, of Japheth, of Ham · Nimrod the first monarch","One language in the world, The building of Babel · The confusion of tongues, The builders of Babel dispersed","God calls Abram, and blesses him with a promise of Christ · Abram departs from Haran","Abram returns out of Egypt with great riches · Strife between the herdsmen of Abram and Lot. Abram gives Lot his choice of the country","The battle of the kings, Lot is taken prisoner · Abram rescues Lot","God encourages Abram · The Divine promise, Abraham is justified by faith","Sarai gives Hagar to Abram · Hagar's misbehaviour to Sarai","God renews the covenant with Abram · Circumcision instituted","The Lord appears to Abraham · Sarah's unbelief reproved","The destruction of Sodom, and the deliverance of Lot · The sin and disgrace of Lot","Abraham's sojourn at Gerar, Sarah is taken by Abimelech · Abimelech's rebuke to Abraham","Birth of Isaac, Sarah's joy · Ishmael mocks Isaac","God commands Abraham to offer up Isaac · Abraham's faith and obedience to the Divine command","The death of Sarah, Abraham applies for a burying-place · Sarah's burying-place","Abraham's care for Isaac's marriage · The journey of Abraham's servant to Mesopotamia, His meeting with Rebekah","Abraham's family by Keturah, His death and burial · God blesses Isaac, The descendants of Ishmael","Isaac, because of famine, goes to Gerar · He denies his wife and is reproved by Abimelech","Isaac sends Esau for venison · Rebekah teaches Jacob to obtain the blessing","Isaac sends Jacob to Padan-aram · Esau marries the daughter of Ishmael","Jacob comes to the well of Haran · His interview with Rachel, Laban entertains him","A further account of Jacob's family · Rachel beareth Joseph","Jacob departs secretly · Laban pursues Jacob","Jacob's vision at Mahanaim, His fear of Esau · Jacob's earnest prayer for deliverance, He prepares a present for Esau","The friendly meeting of Jacob and Esau · Jacob comes to Succoth and Shalem, He builds an altar","Dinah defiled by Shechem · The Shechemites murdered by Simeon and Levi","God commands Jacob to go to Beth-el, He puts away idols from his family · Jacob builds an altar, Death of Deborah, God blesses Jacob","Esau and his descendants","Joseph is loved of Jacob, but hated by his brethren · Joseph's dreams","The profligate conduct of Judah and his family","Joseph preferred by Potiphar · Joseph resists temptation","The chief butler and baker of Pharaoh in prison, Their dreams interpreted by Joseph · The ingratitude of the chief butler","Pharaoh's dreams · Joseph interprets Pharaoh's dreams","Jacob sends ten sons to buy corn · Joseph's treatment of his brethren","Jacob is persuaded to send Benjamin into Egypt · Joseph's reception of his brethren, their fears","Joseph's policy to stay his brethren, and try their affection for Benjamin · Judah's supplication to Joseph","Joseph comforts his brethren, and sends for his father · Pharaoh confirms Joseph's invitation, Joseph's gifts to his brethren","God's promises to Jacob · Jacob and his family go to Egypt","Joseph presents his brethren to Pharaoh · Jacob blesses Pharaoh","Joseph visits his dying father · Jacob blesses Joseph's sons","Jacob calls his sons to bless them · Reuben, Simeon, Levi","The mourning for Jacob · His funeral"]},{"id":1,"name":"Exodus","chapters":40,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The Book of Exodus relates the forming of the children of Israel into a church and a nation. We have hitherto seen true religion shown in domestic life, now, we begin to trace its effects upon the concerns of kingdoms and nations. Exodus signifies “the departure;” the chief event therein recorded is the departure of Israel from Egypt and Egyptian bondage; it plainly points out the fulfilling of several promises and prophecies to Abraham respecting his seed, and shadows forth the state of the church, in the wilderness of this world, until her arrival at the heavenly Canaan, an eternal rest."],"titles":["The children of Israel increase in Egypt after the death of Joseph · They are oppressed, but multiply exceedingly","Moses is born, and exposed on the river · He is found, and brought up by Pharaoh's daughter","God appears to Moses in a burning bush · God sends Moses to deliver Israel","God gives Moses power to work miracles · Moses is loth to be sent, Aaron is to assist him","Pharaoh's displeasure, He increases the tasks of the Israelites · The sufferings of the Israelites, Moses' complaint to God","God renews his promise · Moses and Aaron again sent to Pharaoh","Moses and Aaron encouraged · The rods turned into serpents, Pharaoh's heart is hardened","The plague of frogs · The plague of lice","The murrain of beasts · The plague of boils and blains","The plague of locusts threatened, Pharaoh, moved by his servants, inclines to let the Israelites go · The plague of locusts","God's last instructions to Moses respecting Pharaoh and the Egyptians · The death of the first-born threatened","The beginning of the year changed, The passover instituted · The people instructed how to observe the passover","The first-born sanctified to God, The remembrance of the passover commanded · The firstlings of beasts set apart","God directs the Israelites to Pihahiroth, Pharaoh pursues after them · The Israelites murmur, Moses comforts them","The song of Moses for the deliverance of Israel · The bitter waters at Marah, The Israelites come to Elim","The Israelites come to the wilderness of Sin. They murmur for food, God promises bread from heaven · God sends quails and manna","The Israelites murmur for water at Rephidim, God sendeth it out of the rock · Amalek overcome, The prayers of Moses","Jethro brings to Moses his wife and two sons · Moses entertains Jethro","The people come to Sinai, God's message to them, and their answer · The people directed to prepare to hear the law","The preface to the ten commandments · The commandments of the first table","Laws respecting servants · Judicial laws","Judicial laws","Laws against falsehood and injustice · The year of rest, The sabbath, The three festivals","Moses is called up into the mountain, The people promise obedience · The glory of the Lord appears","What the Israelites were to offer for making the tabernacle · The ark","The curtains of the tabernacle · The curtains of goats' hair","The altar of burnt offerings · The court of the tabernacle","Aaron and his sons set apart for the priest's office, Their garments · The ephod","The sacrifice and ceremony for the consecration of the priests · The continual burnt-offerings, God's promise to dwell among Israel","The altar of incense · The ransom of souls","Bezaleel and Aholiab are appointed and qualified for the work of the tabernacle · The observance of the sabbath","The people cause Aaron to make a golden calf · God's displeasure, The intercession of Moses","The Lord refuses to go with Israel · The tabernacle of Moses removed without the camp","The tables of the law renewed · The name of the Lord proclaimed, The entreaty of Moses","The sabbath to be observed · The free gifts for the tabernacle","The making of the tabernacle, The liberality of the people restrained","The making of the ark, and the furniture of the tabernacle","The brazen altar and laver · The court","The priests' garments · The tabernacle completed","The tabernacle is to be set up, Aaron and his sons to be sanctified · Moses performs all as directed"]},{"id":2,"name":"Leviticus","chapters":27,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["God ordained divers kinds of oblations and sacrifices, to assure his people of the forgiveness of their offences, if they offered them in true faith and obedience. Also he appointed the priests and Levites, their apparel, offices, conduct, and portion. He showed what feasts they should observe, and at what times. He declared by these sacrifices and ceremonies, that the reward of sin is death, and that without the blood of Christ, the innocent Lamb of God, there can be no forgiveness of sins."],"titles":["The offerings · From the herds","The meat-offering of flour · The offering of first-fruits","The peace-offering of the herd · The peace-offering of the flock","The sin-offering of ignorance for the priest · For the whole congregation","Concerning various trespasses · Concerning trespasses against the Lord","Concerning trespasses against our neighbour · Concerning the burnt-offering","Concerning the trespass-offering · Concerning the peace-offering","The consecration of Aaron and his sons · The offerings of consecration","The first offerings of Aaron for himself and the people · Moses and Aaron bless the people, Fire cometh upon the altar from the Lord","The sin and death of Nadab and Abihu · Aaron and his sons forbidden to mourn for Nadab and Abihu","What animals were clean and unclean","Ceremonial purification","Directions to the priest to judge concerning leprosy · Further directions","Of declaring the leper to be clean · The sacrifices to be offered by him","Laws concerning ceremonial uncleanness","The great day of atonement · The sacrifices on it, The scape-goat","All sacrifices to be offered at the tabernacle · Eating of blood, or of animals which died a natural death, forbidden","Unlawful marriages and fleshly lusts","laws","Law against sacrificing children to Moloch, Of children that curse their parents · Laws repeated, Holiness enjoined","Laws concerning the priests","Laws concerning the priests and sacrifices","The feasts of the Lord, The Sabbath · The Passover, The offering of first-fruits","Oil for the lamps, The shew-bread · The law of blasphemy, blasphemer is stoned","The sabbath of rest for the land in the seventh year · The jubilee of the fiftieth year, Oppression forbidden","Promises upon keeping the precepts · Threatenings against disobedience","The law concerning vows, Of persons and animals · Vows concerning houses and land"]},{"id":3,"name":"Numbers","chapters":36,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This book is called NUMBERS from the several numberings of the people contained in it. It extends from the giving of the law at Sinai, till their arrival in the plains of Jordan. An account is given of their murmuring and unbelief, for which they were sentenced to wander in the wilderness nearly forty years; also some laws, both, moral and ceremonial. Their trials greatly tended to distinguish the wicked and hypocrites from the faithful and true servants of God, who served him with a pure heart."],"titles":["The numbering of the Israelites · The number of the people","The order of the tribes in their tents","The sons of Aaron, The Levites taken instead of the first-born · The Levites numbered by their families, Their duties","The Levites' service · The duties of the Kohathites","The unclean to be removed out of the camp, Restitution to be made for trespasses · The trial of jealousy","The law concerning the Nazarites · The form of blessing the people","The offerings of the princes at the dedication of the tabernacle · The offerings of the princes at the dedication of the altar","The lamps of the sanctuary · Consecration of the Levites, and their service","Of the Passover · The removals of the Israelites","The silver trumpets · The Israelites remove from Sinai to Paran","The burning at Taberah · The people lust for flesh, and loathe the manna","God rebukes the murmuring of Aaron and Miriam · Miriam struck with leprosy, and healed at the prayer of Moses","Twelve men sent to search the land of Canaan, Their instructions · Their proceedings","The people murmur at the account of the spies · Joshua and Caleb labour to still the people","The law of the meat-offering and the drink-offering, The stranger under the same law · The sacrifice for the sin of ignorance","The rebellion of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram Korah contends for the priesthood · Disobedience of Dathan and Abiram","Twelve rods laid up before the Lord · Aaron's rod buds, and is kept for a memorial","The charge of the priests and Levites · The priests' portion","The ashes of a heifer · Used to purify the unclean","The people come to Zin, They murmur for water, Moses directed to smite the rock, The infirmity of Moses and Aaron · The Israelites are refused a passage through Edom","The Canaanites of Arad destroyed · The people murmuring, are plagued with fiery serpents, They repenting, are healed through the brazen serpent","Balak's fear of Israel, He sends for Balaam · Balaam goes to Balak","Balak's sacrifice, Balaam pronounces a blessing instead of a curse · Balak's disappointment, and second sacrifice, Balaam again blesses Israel","Balaam, leaving divinations, prophesies the happiness of Israel · Balak dismisses Balaam in anger","The Israelites enticed by the daughters of Moab and Midian · Phinehas puts Zimri and Cozbi to death","Numbering of Israel in the plains of Moab · The division of the land","The daughters of Zelophehad apply for an inheritance, The law of inheritances · Moses warned of his death","Offerings, The daily sacrifice · The offering on the sabbath and new moons","The offering at the feats of trumpets, and on the day of atonement · Offerings at the feast of tabernacles","Vows to be kept · The cases wherein vows might be released","War with Midian · Balaam slain","The tribes of Reuben and Gad request an inheritance on the east of Jordan · Moses reproves the Reubenites and Gadites","Encampments of the Israelites · The Canaanites to be destroyed","The bounds of the promised land · Those appointed to divide the land","The cities of the Levites · The cities of refuge, The laws about murder","The inheritance of the daughters of Zelophehad · The daughters of Zelophehad are to marry in their own tribe"]},{"id":4,"name":"Deuteronomy","chapters":34,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This book repeats much of the history and of the laws contained in the three foregoing books: Moses delivered it to Israel a little before his death, both by word of mouth, that it might affect, and by writing, that it might abide. The men of that generation to which the law was first given were all dead, and a new generation was sprung up, to whom God would have it repeated by Moses himself, now they were going to possess the land of Canaan. The wonderful love of God to his church is set forth in this book; how he ever preserved his church for his own mercies sake, and would still have his name called upon among them. Such are the general outlines of this book, the whole of which shows Moses' love for Israel, and marks him an eminent type of the Lord Jesus Christ. Let us apply the exhortations and persuasions to our own consciences, to excite our minds to a believing, grateful obedience to the commands of God."],"titles":["The words Moses spake to Israel in the plains of Moab, The promise of Canaan · Judges provided for the people","The Edomites to be spared · The Moabites and Ammonites to be spared","The conquest of Og king of Bashan · The land of Gilead and Bashan","Earnest exhortations to obedience, and dissuasives from idolatry · Warnings against disobedience, and promises of mercy","The covenant in Horeb · The ten commandments repeated","A persuasive to obedience · An exhortation to obedience","Intercourse with the Canaanites forbidden · Promises if they were obedient","Exhortations and cautions, enforced by the Lord's former dealings with Israel, and his promises · Exhortations and cautions further enforced","The Israelites not to think their success came by their own worthiness · Moses reminds the Israelites of their rebellions","God's mercies to Israel after their rebellion · An exhortation to obedience","The great work God wrought for Israel · Promises and threatenings","Monuments of idolatry to be destroyed · The place of God's service to be kept","Enticers to idolatry to be put to death · Relations who entice to idolatry not to be spared","The Israelites to distinguish themselves from other nations · Respecting the application of tithes","The year of release · Concerning the release of servants","The yearly feasts · Of judges, Groves and images forbidden","All sacrifices to be perfect, Idolaters must be slain · Difficult controversies","A provision respecting Levites · The abominations of the Canaanites to be avoided","The cities of refuge, The man-slayer, The murderer · Landmarks not to be removed","Exhortation and proclamation respecting those who went to war · Peace to be offered, What cities were to be devoted","The expiation of uncertain murder · Respecting a captive taken to wife","Of humanity towards brethren · Various precepts","Who are shut out from the congregation · Cleanliness enjoined","Of divorce · Of new-married persons, Of man-stealers, Of pledges","Extent of punishment · The ox that treadeth the corn","Confession in offering the first-fruits · The prayer after disposal of the third year's tithe","The law to be written on stones in the promised land · The curses to be pronounced on mount Ebal","The blessings for obedience · The curses for disobedience","Moses calls Israel's mercies to remembrance · The Divine wrath on those who flatter themselves in their wickedness","Mercies promised to the repentant · The commandment manifest","Moses encourages the people, and Joshua · The law to be read every seventh year","The song of Moses · The character of God, The character of Israel","The glorious majesty of God · The blessings of the twelve tribes","Moses views the promised land from mount Nebo · The death and burial of Moses, The mourning of the people"]},{"id":5,"name":"Joshua","chapters":24,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Here is the history of Israel's passing into the land of Canaan, conquering and dividing it, under the command of Joshua, and their history until his death. The power and truth of God in fulfilling his promises to Israel, and in executing his justly threatened vengeance on the Canaanites, are wonderfully displayed. This should teach us to regard the tremendous curses denounced in the word of God against impenitent sinners, and to seek refuge in Christ Jesus."],"titles":["The Lord appoints Joshua to succeed Moses · God promises to assist Joshua","Rahab receives and hides two Israelites · Rahab and the spies","The Israelites come to Jordan · The Lord encourages joshua—Joshua encourages the people","Stones taken out of Jordan · The people pass through Jordan","The Canaanites are afraid, Circumcision renewed · The passover at Gilgal, The manna ceases","The siege of Jericho · The city is compassed","The Israelites smitten at Ai · Joshua's humiliation and prayer","God encourages Joshua · The taking of Ai","The kings combine against Israel · The Gibeonites apply for peace","Five kings war against Gibeon · Joshua succours Gibeon, The sun and moon stand still","Divers kings overcome at the waters of Merom · Hazor is taken and burned","The two kings conquered by Moses · The kings whom Joshua smote","Bounds of the land not yet conquered · Inheritance of Reuben","The nine tribes and a half to have their inheritance · Caleb obtains Hebron","The borders of the lot of Judah · Caleb's portion, His daughter's blessing","The sons of Joseph","The lot of Manasseh · The boundaries of Manasseh, The Canaanites not driven out","The tabernacle set up at Shiloh · The remainder of the land described and divided","The lot of Simeon · The lot of Zebulun","The law concerning the cities of refuge · The cities appointed as refuges","Cities for the Levites · The cities allotted to the Levites","Reuben and Gad, with the half tribe of Manasseh, dismissed to their homes · They build an altar of testimony, The congregation offended thereat","Joshua's exhortation before his death · Joshua warns the people of idolatry","God's benefits to their fathers · Joshua renews the covenant between the people and God"]},{"id":6,"name":"Judges","chapters":21,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The book of Judges is the history of Israel during the government of the Judges, who were occasional deliverers, raised up by God to rescue Israel from their oppressors, to reform the state of religion, and to administer justice to the people. The state of God's people does not appear in this book so prosperous, nor their character so religious, as might have been expected; but there were many believers among them, and the tabernacle service was attended to. The history exemplifies the frequent warnings and predictions of Moses, and should have close attention. The whole is full of important instruction."],"titles":["Proceedings of the tribes of Judah and Simeon · Hebron and other cities taken","The angel of the Lord rebukes the people · The wickedness of the new generation after Joshua","The nations left to prove Israel · Othniel delivers Israel","Israel again revolts, and is oppressed by Jabin · Deborah concerts their deliverance with Barak","Praise and glory ascribed to God · The distress and deliverance of Israel","Israel oppressed by Midianites · Israel rebuked by a prophet","Gideon's army reduced · Gideon is encouraged","Gideon pacifies the Ephraimites · Succoth and Penuel refuse to relieve Gideon","Abimelech murders his brethren, and is made king · Jotham rebukes the Shechemites","Tola and Jair judge Israel · The Philistines and Ammonites oppress Israel","Jephtah and the Gileadites · He attempts to make peace","Ephraimites quarrel with Jephthah · Ibzan, Elon, and Abdon judge Israel","The Philistines, Samson announced · The angel appears to Manoah","Samson desires a wife of the Philistines · Samson kills a lion","Samson is denied his wife, He smites the Philistines · Samson kills a thousand of the Philistines with a jaw-bone","Samson's escape from Gaza · Samson enticed to declare his strength lay","The beginning of idolatry in Israel, Micah and his mother · Micah hires a Levite to be his priest","The Danites seek to enlarge their inheritance, and rob Micah","The wickedness of the men of Gibeah","The tribe of Benjamin nearly extirpated","The Israelites lament for the Benjamites"]},{"id":7,"name":"Ruth","chapters":4,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["We find in this book excellent examples of faith, piety, patience, humility, industry, and loving-kindness, in the common events of life. Also we see the special care which God's providence take of our smallest concerns, encouraging us to full trust therein. We may view this book as a beautiful, because natural representation of human life; as a curious detail of important facts; and as a part of the plan of redemption."],"titles":["Elimelech and his sons die in the land of Moab · Naomi returns home","Ruth gleans in the field of Boaz · The kindness of Boaz to Ruth","The directions given to Ruth by Naomi · Boaz acknowledges the duty of a kinsman","The kinsman refuses to redeem Ruth's inheritance · Boaz marries Ruth"]},{"id":8,"name":"1 Samuel","chapters":31,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["In this book we have an account of Eli, and the wickedness of his sons; also of Samuel, his character and actions. Then of the advancement of Saul to be the king of Israel, and his ill behaviour, until his death made way for David's succession to the throne, who was an eminent type of Christ. David's patience, modesty, constancy, persecution by open enemies and feigned friends, are a pattern and example to the church, and to every member of it. Many things in this book encourage the faith, hope, and patience of the suffering believer. It contains also many useful cautions and awful warnings."],"titles":["Elkanah and his family · Hannah's prayer","Hannah's song of thanksgiving · The wickedness of Eli's sons, Samuel's ministry","The word of the Lord first revealed to Samuel · God tells Samuel the destruction of Eli's house","The Israelites overcome by the Philistines · The ark taken","Dagon is broken before the ark · The Philistine smitten","The Philistines consult how to send back the ark · They bring it to Bethshemesh","The ark removed to Kirjath-jearim · The Israelites solemnly repent","The evil government of Samuel's sons · The Israelites ask for a king","Saul is brought to Samuel · Samuel told concerning Saul","Samuel anoints Saul · Saul prophesies","Jabesh-gilead delivered · Saul confirmed in his kingdom","Samuel testifies his integrity · Samuel reproves the people","The invasion of the Philistines · Saul sacrifices, He is reproved by Samuel","Jonathan smites the Philistines · Their defeat","Saul sent to destroy Amalek · Saul excuses and commends himself","Samuel sent to Bethlehem to Jesse · David is anointed","Goliath's challenge · David comes to the camp","Jonathan's friendship for David · Saul seeks to kill David","Jonathan reconciles his father to David, Saul again tries to slay him · David flees to Samuel","David consults Jonathan · Jonathan's covenant with David","David with Ahimelech · David at Gath feigns himself mad","David at Adullam, Many resort to him · Saul destroys the priests of Nob","David rescues Keilah · God warns him to escape from Keilah","David spares Saul's life · David shows his innocence","Death of Samuel · David's request; Nabal's churlish refusal","Saul goes after David, who again spares Saul's life · David exhorts Saul","David retires to Gath · David deceives Achish","Achish puts confidence in David, Saul's fear · Saul consults a witch at Endor","David objected to by the Philistines · He is dismissed by Achish","Ziklag spoiled by the Amalekites · David overtakes the Amalekites","Saul's defeat and death · Saul's body rescued by the men of Jabesh-gilead"]},{"id":9,"name":"2 Samuel","chapters":24,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This book is the history of the reign of king David. It relates his victories, the growth of the prosperity of Israel, and his reformation of the state of religion. With these events are recorded the grievous sins he committed, and the family as well as public troubles with which he was punished. We here meet with many things worthy of imitation, and many that are written for our warning. The history of king David is given in Scripture with much faithfulness, and from it he appears, to those who fairly balance his many virtues and excellent qualities against his faults, to have been a great and good man."],"titles":["Tidings brought to David of the death of Saul · The Amalekite is put to death","David made king in Hebron · Abner makes Ishbosheth king Battle between Abner's men and those of Joab","David's power increases His family · Abner revolts to David","Ishbosheth murdered · David puts to death the murderers","David king over all Israel · He takes the strong-hold of Zion","The ark removed from Kirjath-jearim · Uzzah smitten for touching the ark, Obed-edom blessed","David's care for the ark · God's covenant with David","David subdues the Philistines, the Moabites, and the Syrians · The spoil dedicated","David sends for Mephibosheth · And provides for him","David's messengers ill-treated by Hanun · The Ammonites defeated","David's adultery · He tries to conceal his crime","Nathan's parable—David confesses his sin · The birth of Solomon","Ammon's violence to his sister · Absalom murders his brother Ammon","Joab procures Absalom's recall · Absalom recalled","Absalom's ambition · His conspiracy","Ziba's falsehood · David cursed by Shimei","Ahithophel's counsel overthrown · He hangs himself, Absalom pursues David","Absalom's army defeated · He is slain","Joab causes David to cease mourning · David returns to Jordan","Sheba's rebellion · Amasa slain by Joab","The Gibeonites avenged · Rizpah's care for the bodies of Saul's descendants","David's psalm of thanksgiving","David's last words · David's mighty men","David numbers the people · He chooses the pestilence"]},{"id":10,"name":"1 Kings","chapters":22,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The history now before us accounts for the affairs of the kingdoms of Judah and Israel, yet with special regard to the kingdom of God among them; for it is a sacred history. It is earlier as to time, teaches much more, and is more interesting than any common histories."],"titles":["David's declining age · Adonijah aspires to the throne","David's dying charge to Solomon · David's charge as to Joab and others","Solomon's marriage · His vision, His prayer for wisdom","Solomon's court · Solomon's dominions, His daily provision","Solomon's agreement with Hiram · Solomon's workmen for the temple","The building of Solomon's temple · Promise given concerning the temple","Solomon's buildings · Furniture of the temple","The dedication of the temple · The occasion","God's answer to Solomon · The presents of Solomon and Hiram","The queen of Sheba's visit to Solomon · Solomon's wealth","Solomon's wives and concubines, His idolatry · God's anger","Rehoboam's accession, The people's petition, His rough answer · Ten tribes revolt","Jeroboam's sin reproved · The prophet deceived","Abijah being sick, his mother consults Ahijah · The destruction of Jeroboam's house","Wicked reign of Abijam, king of Judah · Good reign of Asa, king of Judah","The reigns of Baasha and Elah in Israel · Reigns of Zimri and Omri in Israel","Elijah fed by ravens · Elijah sent to Zarephath","Elijah sends Ahab notice of his coming · Elijah meets Ahab","Elijah flees to the wilderness · God manifests himself to Elijah","Benhadad besieges Samaria · Benhadad's defeat","Ahab covets Naboth's vineyard · Naboth murdered by Jezebel","Jehoshaphat makes a league with Ahab · Micaiah predicts the death of Ahab"]},{"id":11,"name":"2 Kings","chapters":25,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":[],"titles":["The revolt of Moab—Sickness of Ahaziah, king of Israel · Fire called from heaven by Elijah—Death of Ahaziah","Elijah divides Jordan · Elijah is taken up into heaven","Jehoram, king of Israel · War with Moab, The intercession of Elisha","Elisha multiplies the widow's oil · The Shunammite obtains a son","Naaman's leprosy · The cure of it","The sons of the prophets enlarge their habitations, Iron made to swim · Elisha discloses the counsels of the Syrians","Elisha prophesies plenty · The flight of the Syrian army","A famine in Israel, The Shunammite obtains her land · Elisha consulted by Hazael, Death of Benhadad","Elisha sends to anoint Jehu · Jehu and the captains","Ahab's sons and Ahaziah's brethren put to death · Jehu destroys the worshippers of Baal","Athaliah usurps the government of Judah, Jehoash made king · Athaliah put to death","Jehoash orders the repair of the temple · He is slain by his servants","Reign of Jehoahaz · Jehoash, king of Israel, Elisha dying","Amaziah's good reign · Amaziah provokes Jehoash king of Israel, and is overcome","Reign of Azariah, or Uzziah, king of Judah · The latter kings of Israel","Ahaz, king of Judah, His wicked reign · Ahaz takes a pattern from an idol's altar","Reign of Hoshea in Israel, The israelites carried captives by the Assyrians · Captivity of the Israelites","Good reign of Hezekiah in Judah, Idolatry · Sennacherib invades Judah","Hezekiah receives an answer of peace · Sennacherib's letter","Hezekiah's sickness, His recovery in answer to prayer · Hezekiah shows his treasures to the ambassadors from Babylon, His death","Wicked reign of Manasseh · The prophetic denunciations against Judah","Josiah's good reign, His care for repairing the temple, The book of the law found · Josiah consults Huldah the prophetess","Josiah reads the law, and renews the covenant · He destroys idolatry","Jehoiakim subdued by Nebuchadnezzar · Jehoiachim captive in Babylon","Jerusalem besieged, Zedekiah taken · The temple burnt, The people carried into captivity"]},{"id":12,"name":"1 Chronicles","chapters":29,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The books of Chronicles are, in a great measure, repetitions of what is in the books of Samuel and of the Kings, yet there are some excellent useful things in them which we find not elsewhere. The FIRST BOOK traces the rise of the Jewish people from Adam, and afterward gives an account of the reign of David. In the SECOND BOOK the narrative is continued, and relates the progress and end of the kingdom of Judah; also it notices the return of the Jews from the Babylonish captivity. Jerome says, that whoever supposes himself to have knowledge of the Scriptures without being acquainted with the books of Chronicles, deceives himself. Historical facts passed over elsewhere, names, and the connexion of passages are to be found here, and many questions concerning the gospel are explained."],"titles":["Genealogies, Adam to Abraham · The descendants of Abraham","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","Genealogies","The death of Saul","David raised to the throne · A list of David's mighty men","Those who came to David at Ziklag · Those who came to Hebron","David consults about the ark · The removal of the ark","David's victories","Preparations for the removal of the ark · The removal of the ark","The solemnity with which the ark was fixed · David's psalm of praise","David's purposes; God's gracious promises","David's victories","David's wars","David's wars","David's numbering the people","David's preparations for the temple · David's instructions to Solomon","David declares Solomon his successor · The office of the Levites","The divisions of the priests and Levites","The singers and musicians","The offices of the Levites","David's military force · Princes and officers","David exhorts the people to the fear of the Lord · He gives instructions for the temple","David induces the princes and people to offer willingly · His thanksgiving and prayer"]},{"id":13,"name":"2 Chronicles","chapters":36,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":[],"titles":["Solomon's choice of wisdom, His strength and wealth","Solomon's message to Huram respecting the temple, His treaty with Huram","The building of the temple","The furniture of the temple","The ark placed in the temple · The temple filled with glory","Solomon's prayer at the dedication of the temple","God's answer to Solomon's prayer","Solomon's buildings and trade","The queen of Sheba · Solomon's riches, and his death","The ten tribes revolt from Rehoboam","Rehoboam forbidden to war against Israel · The priests and Levites find refuge in Judah","Rehoboam, forsaking the Lord, is punished","Abijah overcomes Jeroboam","Asa's piety, He strengthens his kingdom","The people make a solemn covenant with God","Asa seeks the aid of the Syrians, His death","Jehoshaphat promotes religion in Judah, His prosperity","Jehoshaphat's alliance with Ahab","Jehoshaphat visits his kingdom","The danger and distress of Judah · Jahaziel's prophecy of victory","The wicked reign of Jehoram · Jehoram's miserable end","The reign of Ahaziah, Athaliah destroys the royal family","Joash crowned, and Athaliah slain","Joash, of Judah, The temple repaired · Joash falls into idolatry, He is slain by his servants","Amaziah, king of Judah · Amaziah worships the idols of Edom","Uzziah's good reign in Judah · Uzziah's attempt to burn incense","Jotham's reign in Judah","The wicked reign of Ahaz in Judah","Hezekiah's good reign in Judah · Hezekiah's sacrifice of atonement","Hezekiah's passover · The passover celebrated","Hezekiah destroys idolatry","The invasion of Sennacherib, His defeat · Hezekiah's sickness, His prosperous reign, and death","Manasseh's and repentance · Amon's wicked reign in Judah","Josiah's good reign in Judah","The passover kept by Josiah · Josiah slain in battle","The destruction of Jerusalem · The proclamation of Cyrus"]},{"id":14,"name":"Ezra","chapters":10,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The history of this book is the accomplishment of Jeremiah's prophecy concerning the return of the Jews out of Babylon. From its contents we especially learn, that every good work will meet with opposition from enemies, and be hurt by the misconduct of friends; but that God will make his cause to prevail, notwithstanding all obstacles and adversaries. The restoration of the Jews was an event of the highest consequence, tending to preserve religion in the world, and preparing the way for the appearance of the Great Deliverer, the Lord Jesus Christ."],"titles":["The proclamation of Cyrus for the rebuilding of the temple · The people provide for their return","The numbers that returned · The numbers of the priests and Levites","The altar and festivals · The foundations of the temple laid","The adversaries of the temple · The building of the temple is hindered","The leaders forward the building of the temple · letter against the Jews","The decree for completing the temple · The temple is finished","Ezra goes up to Jerusalem · The commission to Ezra","The companions of Ezra · Ezra implores God's blessing","Ezra mourns for the Jews' conduct · Ezra's confession of sins","Ezra encourages to reformation · He assembles the people"]},{"id":15,"name":"Nehemiah","chapters":13,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The Old Testament history closes with the book of Nehemiah, wherein is recorded the workings of his heart, in the management of public affairs; with many devout reflections."],"titles":["Nehemiah's distress for the misery of Jerusalem, His prayer","Nehemiah's request to the king · Nehemiah comes to Jerusalem","The rebuilding the walls of Jerusalem","Opposition of Sanballat and others · The designs of the adversaries","The Jews complain of grievances · Nehemiah redresses the grievances","Sanballat's plot to hinder Nehemiah · False prophets try to frighten Nehemiah","The city committed to Hananiah · Register of those that first returned","The reading and expounding the law · The people called upon to be joyful","A solemn fast · Prayer and confession of sin","The covenant, Those who signed it · Their engagement to sacred rites","The distribution of the people","The priests and Levites that returned · The dedication of the wall","Nehemiah turns out the mixed multitude · Nehemiah's reform in the house of God"]},{"id":16,"name":"Esther","chapters":10,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["We find in this book, that even those Jews who were scattered in the province of the heathen, were taken care of, and were wonderfully preserved, when threatened with destruction. Though the name of God be not in this book, the finger of God is shown by minute events for the bringing about his people's deliverance. This history comes in between Ezr 6; 7."],"titles":["The royal feast of Ahasuerus · Vashti's refusal to appear, The king's decree","Esther chosen queen · Mordecai discovers a plot against the king","Haman seeks to destroy the Jews · He obtains a decree against the Jews","The Jews lament their danger · Esther undertakes to plead for the Jews","Esther's application received · Haman prepares to hang Mordecai","Providence recommends Mordecai to the king's favour · Haman's counsel honours Mordecai","Esther accuses Haman · Haman hanged on his own gallows","Mordecai is advanced · Esther makes suit for the Jews","The success of the Jews · The feast of Purim in remembrance of this","Greatness of Ahasuerus—Mordecai's advancement"]},{"id":17,"name":"Job","chapters":42,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This book is so called from Job, whose prosperity, afflictions, and restoration, are here recorded. He lived soon after Abraham, or perhaps before that patriarch. Most likely it was written by Job himself, and it is the most ancient book in existence. The instructions to be learned from the patience of Job, and from his trials, are as useful now, and as much needed as ever. We live under the same Providence, we have the same chastening Father, and there is the same need for correction unto righteousness. The fortitude and patience of Job, though not small, gave way in his severe troubles; but his faith was fixed upon the coming of his Redeemer, and this gave him stedfastness and constancy, though every other dependence, particularly the pride and boast of a self-righteous spirit, was tried and consumed. Another great doctrine of the faith, particularly set forth in the book of Job, is that of Providence. It is plain, from this history, that the Lord watched over his servant Job with the affection of a wise and loving father."],"titles":["The piety and prosperity of Job · Satan obtains leave to try Job","Satan obtains leave to try Job · Job's sufferings","Job complains that he was born · Job complaining","Eliphaz reproves Job · And maintains that God's judgments are for the wicked","Eliphaz urges that the sin of sinners in their ruin · God is to be regarded in affliction","Job justifies his complaints · He wishes for death","Job's troubles · Job expostulates with God","Bildad reproves Job · Hypocrites will be destroyed","Job acknowledges God's justice · He is not able to contend with God","Job complains of his hardships · He pleads with God as his Maker","Zophar reproves Job · God's perfections and almighty power","Job reproves his friends · The wicked often prosper","Job reproves his friends · He professes his confidence in God","Job speaks of man's life · Of man's death","Eliphaz reproves Job · The unquietness of wicked men","Job reproves his friends · He represents his case as deplorable","Job appeals from man to God · His hope is not in life, but in death","Bildad reproves Job · Ruin attends the wicked","Job complains of unkind usage · God was the Author of his afflictions","Zophar speaks of the short joy of the wicked · The ruin of the wicked","Job entreats attention · The prosperity of the wicked","Eliphaz shows that a man's goodness profits not God · Job accused of oppression","Job complains that God has withdrawn · He asserts his own integrity","Wickedness often unpunished · The wicked shun the light","Bildad shows that man cannot be justified before God","Job reproves Bildad · Job acknowledges the power of God","Job protests his sincerity · The hypocrite is without hope","Concerning wordly wealth · Wisdom is of inestimable value","Job's former comforts · The honour paid to Job, His usefulness","Job's honour is turned into contempt · Job a burden to himself","Job declares his uprightness · His integrity","Elihu is displeased at the dispute between Job and his friends · He reproves them","Elihu offers to reason with Job · Elihu blames Job for reflecting upon God","Elihu accuses Job of charging God with injustice · God cannot be unjust","Elihu speaks of man's conduct · Why those who cry out under afflictions are not regarded","Elihu desires Job's attention · The methods in which God deals with men","Elihu observes the power of God · Job required to explain the works of nature","God calls upon Job to answer · God questions Job","God inquires of Job concerning several animals","Job humbles himself to God · The Lord reasons with Job to show his righteousness, power, and wisdom","Concerning Leviathan","Job humbly submits unto God · Job intercedes for his friends"]},{"id":18,"name":"Psalms","chapters":150,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["David was the penman of most of the psalms, but some evidently were composed by other writers, and the writers of some are doubtful. But all were written by the inspiration of the Holy Ghost; and no part of the Old Testament is more frequently quoted or referred to in the New. Every psalm either points directly to Christ, in his person, his character, and offices; or may lead the believer's thoughts to Him. And the psalms are the language of the believer's heart, whether mourning for sin, thirsting after God, or rejoicing in Him. Whether burdened with affliction, struggling with temptation, or triumphing in the hope or enjoyment of deliverance; whether admiring the Divine perfections, thanking God for his mercies, mediating on his truths, or delighting in his service; they form a Divinely appointed standard of experience, by which we may judge ourselves. Their value, in this view, is very great, and the use of them will increase with the growth of the power of true religion in the heart. By the psalmist's expressions, the Spirit helps us to pray. If we make the psalms familiar to us, whatever we have to ask at the throne of grace, by way of confession, petition, or thanksgiving, we may be assisted from thence. Whatever devout affection is working in us, holy desire or hope, sorrow or joy, we may here find words to clothe it; sound speech which cannot be condemned. In the language of this Divine book, the prayers and praises of the church have been offered up to the throne of grace from age to age."],"titles":["The holiness and happiness of a godly man · The sinfulness and misery of a wicked man, The ground and reason of both","Threatenings against the enemies of Christ's kingdom · Promise to Christ as the Head of this kingdom","David complains to God of his enemies, and confides in God · He triumphs over his fears, and gives God the glory, and takes to himself the comfort","The children of men proved, and the happiness of godly people · God's favour is happiness","God will certainly hear prayer: David gives to God the glory, and takes to himself the comfort · He prayed for himself, that God would guide him, and for all the Lord's people, that God would give them joy, and keep them safe","The psalmist deprecates God's wrath, and begs for the return of his favour · He assures himself of an answer of peace","The psalmist prays to God to plead his cause, and judge for him · He expresses confidence in God, and will give him the glory of his deliverance","God is to be glorified, for making known himself to us · And for making even the heavenly bodies useful to man, thereby placing him but little lower than the angels","David praises God for protecting his people · And for cause to praise him","The psalmist complains of the wickedness of the wicked · He prays to God to appear for the relief of his people","Chapter explanation","The psalmist begs help of God, because there were none among men whom he durst trust","The psalmist complains that God had long withdrawn. He earnestly prays for comfort. He assures himself of an answer of peace","A description of the depravity of human nature, and the deplorable corruption of a great part of mankind","The way to heaven, if we would be happy, we must be holy. We are encouraged to walk in that way","Chapter explanation","David's integrity · The character of his enemies. His hope of happiness","David rejoices in the deliverances God wrought for him · He takes the comfort of his integrity, which God had cleared up","The glory of God's works · His holiness and grace as shown in his word","This psalm is a prayer for the kings of Israel, but with relation to Christ","Thanksgiving for victory · Confidence of further success","Complaints of discouragement · With prayer for deliverance","Confidence in God's grace and care","Concerning the kingdom of Christ, and the subjects of that kingdom · Concerning the King of that kingdom","Confidence in prayer · Prayer for remission of sins","David, in this psalm, appeals to God touching his integrity","The psalmist's faith · His desire toward God, and expectation from him","A prayer in distress · Thanksgiving for deliverance","Exhortation to give glory to God","Praise to God for deliverance · Others encouraged by his example","Confidence in God · Prayer in trouble","The happiness of a pardoned sinner · The misery that went before, and the comfort that followed the confession of sins","God to be praised · His people encouraged by his power","David praises God, and encourages to trust him · He exhorts to fear","David prays for safety · He complains of his enemies","The bad state of the wicked · The goodness of God","Verses 1–6 · Verses 7–20","God's displeasure at sin · The psalmist's sufferings and prayers","David meditates on man's frailty · He applies for pardon and deliverance","Confidence for deliverance · Christ's work of redemption","God's care for his people · The treachery of David's enemies","Verses 1–5 · Verses 6–11","David endeavours to still his spirit, with hope and confidence in God","Verses 1–8 · Verses 9–16","Verses 1–5 · Verses 6–9","Confidence in God · An exhortation to behold it","Verses 1–4 · Verses 5–9","Verses 1–7 · Verses 8–14","A call for attention · Folly of worldlings","The glory of God · Sacrifices to be changed for prayers","The psalmist prays for mercy, humbly confessing and lamenting his sins · He pleads for pardon, that he may promote the glory of God and the conversion of sinners","The enemies of the truth and the church described, Their destruction · The righteous rejoice","The corruption of man by nature","David complains of the malice of his enemies · Assurance of the Divine favour and protection","Prayer to God to manifest his favour · The great wickedness and treachery of his enemies","David seeks mercy from God, amidst the malice of his enemies · He rests his faith on God's promises, and declares his obligation to praise him for mercies","David begins with prayer and complaint · He concludes with joy and praise","Wicked judges described and reproved · A prayer that they may be disabled, and their ruin predicted","David prays for deliverance from his enemies · He foresees their destruction","David prays for the deliverance of Israel from their enemies · He entreats God to carry on and complete their victories","David seeks God upon former experience · He vows to serve God","David's confidence in God · No trust to be put in worldly things","David's desire toward God · His satisfaction in God","Prayer for deliverance · The destruction of the wicked, encouragement to the righteous","God is to be praised in the kingdom of grace · In the kingdom of providence","Praise for God's sovereign power in the creation · For his favour to his church","A prayer for the enlargement of Christ's kingdom","A prayer—The greatness and goodness of God · The wonderful works God wrought for his people","David complains of great distress · And begs for succour","The speedy destruction of the wicked, and the preservation of the godly","Prayers that God would deliver and save · Believing praises","David begins with a prayer for Solomon · He passes into a prophecy of the glories of his reign, and of Christ's kingdom","The psalmist's temptation · How he gained a victory over it","The desolations of the sanctuary · Pleas for encouraging faith","The psalmist declares his resolution of executing judgment · He rebukes the wicked, and concludes with resolutions to praise God","The psalmist speaks of God's power · All have to fear and to trust in him","The psalmist's troubles and temptation · He encourages himself by the remembrance of God's help of his people","Attention called for · The history of Israel","The deplorable condition of the people of God · A petition for relief","The psalmist complains of the miseries of the church · Its former prosperity and present desolation","God is praised for what he has done for his people · Their obligations to him","An exhortation to judges · The doom of evil rulers","The designs of the enemies of Israel · Earnest prayer for their defeat","The psalmist expresses his affection to the ordinances of God · His desire towards the God of the ordinances","Prayers for the continuance of former mercies · Trust in God's goodness","The psalmist pleads his earnestness, and the mercy of God, as reasons why his prayer should be heard · He renews his requests for help and comfort","The glory of the church · It is filled with the Divine blessing","The psalmist pours out his soul to God in lamentation · He wrestles by faith, in his prayer to God for comfort","God's mercy and truth, and his covenant · The glory and perfection of God","The eternity of God, the frailty of man · Submission to Divine chastisements","The safety of those who have God for their refuge · Their favour with Him","Praise is the business of the sabbath · The wicked shall perish, but God's people shall be exalted","The majesty, power, and holiness of Christ's kingdom","The danger and folly of persecutors · Comfort and peace to the persecuted","part. An exhortation to praise God · A warning not to tempt Him","A call to all people to praise God · God's government and judgment","The Lord Jesus reigns in power that cannot be resisted · His care of his people, and his provision for them","The glory of the Redeemer · The joy of the Redeemer","The happy government God's people are under · Its happy administration","An exhortation to praise God, and rejoice in him","David's vow and profession of godliness","A sorrowful complaint of great afflictions · Encouragement by expecting the performances of God's promises to his church","An exhortation to bless God for his mercy · And to the church and to all men","God's majesty in the heavens, The creation of the sea, and the dry land · His provision for all creatures","A solemn call to praise and serve the Lord · His gracious dealings with Israel","The happiness of God's people · Israel's sins","God's providential care of the children of men in distresses, in banishment, and dispersion · In captivity","Chapter explanation","David complains of his enemies · He prophesies their destruction","Christ's kingdom","The Lord is to be praised for his works","The blessedness of the righteous","An exhortation to praise God","An exhortation to fear God","Glory to be ascribed to God · by trusting in him and praising him","The psalmist declares his love to the Lord · His desire to be thankful","All people called upon to praise God","It is good to trust in the Lord · The coming of Christ in his kingdom","Verses 1–8 · Verses 9–16","The psalmist prays to God to deliver him from false and malicious tongues · He complains of wicked neighbours","The safety of the godly","Esteem for Jerusalem · Concern for its welfare","Confidence in God under contempt","The deliverance of the church · Thankfulness for the deliverance","The security of the righteous · Prayer for them, The ruin of the wicked","Those returned out of captivity are to be thankful · Those yet in captivity are encouraged","The value of the Divine blessing","The blessings of those who fear God","Thankfulness for former deliverances · A believing prospect of the destruction of the enemies of Zion","The psalmist's hope in prayer · His patience in hope","The psalmist's humility. Believers encouraged to trust in God","David's care for the ark · The promises of God","The excellency of brotherly love","An exhortation to bless the Lord","God to be praised for his mercy · For his power and judgments","God to be praised as the Creator of the world · As Israel's God and Saviour","The Jews bewail their captivity · Their affection for Jerusalem","The psalmist praises God for answering prayer · The Lord's dealing with the humble and the proud","God knows all things · He is every where present","David encourages himself in God · He prays for, and prophesies the destruction of, his persecutors","David prays for God's acceptance and assistance · That God would appear for his rescue","David's comfort in prayer","David complains of his enemies and distresses · He prays for comfort, guidance, and deliverance","David acknowledges the great goodness of God, and prays for help · He prays for the prosperity of his kingdom","David extols the power, goodness, and mercy of the Lord · The glory of God's kingdom, and his care of those that love him","Why we should not trust in men · Why we should trust in God","The people of God are exhorted to praise him for his mercies and care · For the salvation and prosperity of the church","The creatures placed in the upper world called on to praise the Lord · Also the creatures of this lower world, especially his own people","Joy to all the people of God · Terror to their enemies","A psalm of praise"]},{"id":19,"name":"Proverbs","chapters":31,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The subject of this book may be thus stated by an enlargement on the opening verses. 1. The Proverbs of Solomon, the son of David, king of Israel. 2. Which treat of the knowledge of wisdom, of piety towards God, of instruction and moral discipline, of the understanding wise and prudent counsels. 3. Which treat of the attainment of instruction in wisdom, which wisdom is to be shown in the conduct of life, and consists in righteousness with regard to our fellow-creatures. 4. Which treat of the giving to the simple sagacity to discover what is right, by supplying them with just principles, and correct views of virtue and vice; and to the young man knowledge, so that he need not err through ignorance; and discretion, so that by pondering well these precepts, he may not err through obstinacy. Take the proverbs of other nations, and we shall find great numbers founded upon selfishness, cunning, pride, injustice, national contempt, and animosities. The principles of the Proverbs of Solomon are piety, charity, justice, benevolence, and true prudence. Their universal purity proves that they are the word of God."],"titles":["The use of the Proverbs · Exhortations to fear God and obey parents","Promises to those who seek wisdom · The advantages of wisdom","Exhortations to obedience and faith · To piety, and to improve afflictions","Exhortation to the study of wisdom · Cautions against bad company, Exhortation to faith and holiness","Exhortations to wisdom. The evils of licentiousness · Remedies against licentiousness, The miserable end of the wicked","Cautions against rash suretiship · A rebuke to slothfulness","Invitations to learn wisdom · The arts of seducers, with warnings against them","Christ, as Wisdom, calls to the sons of men · The nature and riches of Wisdom","The invitations of Wisdom · The invitations of folly","Verse 1 · Verses 2, 3","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verses 1–3 · Verses 4, 5","Verses 1, 2 · Verses 3–6","Verses 1–3 · Verses 4, 5","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verse 1 · Verse 2","Verses 1–6 · Verses 7–9","An exhortation to king Lemuel to take heed of sin, and to do duties · The description of a virtuous woman"]},{"id":20,"name":"Ecclesiastes","chapters":12,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The name of this book signifies \"The Preacher.\" The wisdom of God here preaches to us, speaking by Solomon, who it is evident was the author. At the close of his life, being made sensible of his sin and folly, he recorded here his experience for the benefit of others, as the book of his repentance; and he pronounced all earthly good to be \"vanity and vexation of spirit.\" It convinces us of the vanity of the world, and that it cannot make us happy; of the vileness of sin, and its certain tendency to make us miserable. It shows that no created good can satisfy the soul, and that happiness is to be found in God alone; and this doctrine must, under the blessed Spirit's teaching, lead the heart to Christ Jesus."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The changes of human affairs. (Eccl. 3:1-10) The Divine counsels unchangeable. (Eccl. 3:11-15) The vanity of worldly power. (Eccl. 3:16-22)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Exhortation to liberality. (Eccl. 11:1-6) An admonition to prepare for death, and to young persons to be religious. (Eccl. 11:7-10)","A description of the infirmities of age. (Eccl. 12:1-7) All is vanity: also a warning of the judgment to come. (Eccl. 12:8-14)"]},{"id":21,"name":"Song of Solomon","chapters":8,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This book is a Divine allegory, which represents the love between Christ and his church of true believers, under figures taken from the relation and affection that subsist between a bridegroom and his espoused bride; an emblem often employed in Scripture, as describing the nearest, firmest, and most sure relation: see Ps. 45; Is. 54:5,6; 62:5; Jer. 2:2; 3:1; also in Ezekiel, Hosea, and by our Lord himself, Matt. 9:15; 25:1: see also Rev. 21:2,9; Eph. 5:27. There is no character in the church of Christ, and no situation in which the believer is placed, but what may be traced in this book, as humble inquirers will find, on comparing it with other Scriptures, by the assistance of God the Holy Spirit, in answer to their supplications. Much, however, of the language has been misunderstood by expositors and translators. The difference between the customs and manners of Europe, and those of the East, must especially be kept in view. The little acquaintance with eastern customs possessed by most of our early expositors and translators, has in many cases prevented a correct rendering. Also, the changes in our own language, during the last two or three centuries, affect the manner in which some expressions are viewed, and they must not be judged by modern notions. But the great outlines, rightly interpreted, fully accord with the affections and experience of the sincere Christian."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The trials of the church by the withdrawing of Christ. (Song 3:1-5) The excellences of the church, The care of Christ for her. (Song 3:6-11)","Chapter explanation","Christ's answer. (Song 5:1) The disappointments of the church from her own folly. (Song 5:2-8) The excellences of Christ. (Song 5:9-16)","Chapter explanation","The graces of the church. (Song 7:1-9) The delight of the church in Christ. (Song 7:10-13)","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":22,"name":"Isaiah","chapters":66,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Isaiah prophesied in the reigns of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah. He has been well called the evangelical prophet, on account of his numerous and full prophesies concerning the coming and character, the ministry and preaching, the sufferings and death of the Messiah, and the extent and continuance of his kingdom. Under the veil of the deliverance from Babylon, Isaiah points to a much greater deliverance, which was to be effected by the Messiah; and seldom does he mention the one, without alluding at the same time to the other; nay, he is often so much enraptured with the prospect of the more distant deliverance, as to lose sight of that which was nearer, and to dwell on the Messiah's person, office, character, and kingdom."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","The conversion of the Gentiles, Description of the sinfulness of Israel. (Is. 2:1-9) The awful punishment of unbelievers. (Is. 2:10-22)","Chapter explanation","The havoc occasioned by war. (Is. 4:1) The times of the Messiah. (Is. 4:2-6)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Exhortations and warnings. (Is. 8:1-8) Comfort for those who fear God. (Is. 8:9-16) Afflictions to idolaters. (Is. 8:17-22)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The peaceful character of Christ's kingdom and subjects. (Is. 11:1-9) The conversion of the Gentiles and Jews. (Is. 11:10-16)","This is a hymn of praise suited to the times of the Messiah","The armies of God's wrath. (Is. 13:1-5) The conquest of Babylon. (Is. 13:6-18) Its final desolation. (Is. 13:19-22)","Chapter explanation","The Divine judgments about to come upon the Moabites","Moab is exhorted to yield obedience. (Is. 16:1-5) The pride and the judgments of Moab. (Is. 16:6-14)","Syria and Israel threatened. (Is. 17:1-11) The woe of Israel's enemies. (Is. 17:12-14)","God's care for his people; and the increase of the church","Judgments upon Egypt. (Is. 19:1-17) Its deliverance, and the conversion of the people. (Is. 19:18-25)","The invasion and conquest of Egypt and Ethiopia","The taking of Babylon. (Is. 21:1-10) Of the Edomites. (Is. 21:11,12) Of the Arabs. (Is. 21:13-17)","Chapter explanation","The overthrow of Tyre. (Is. 23:1-14) It is established again. (Is. 23:15-18)","The desolation of the land. (Is. 24:1-12) A few shall be preserved. (Is. 24:13-15) God's kingdom advanced by his judgments. (Is. 24:16-23)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","God's care over his people. (Is. 27:1-5) A promise of their recall to Divine favour. (Is. 27:6-13)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The sin and folly of seeking help from Egypt. (Is. 31:1-5) God's care for Jerusalem. (Is. 31:6-9)","Times of peace and happiness. (Is. 32:1-8) An interval of trouble, yet comfort and blessings in the end. (Is. 32:9-20)","God's judgments against the enemies of his church. (Is. 33:1-14) The happiness of his people. (Is. 33:15-24)","God's vengeance against the enemies of his church. (Is. 34:1-8) Their desolation. (Is. 34:9-17)","The flourishing state of Christ's kingdom. (Is. 35:1-4) The privileges of his people. (Is. 35:5-10)","See II Kin. 18:17-37, and the commentary thereon","This chapter is the same as II Kin. 19","Hezekiah's sickness and recovery. (Is. 38:1-8) His thanksgiving. (Is. 38:9-22)","This chapter is the same as II Kin. 20:12-19","Chapter explanation","God's care of his people. (Is. 41:1-9) they are encouraged not to fear. (Is. 41:10-20) The vanity and folly of idolatry. (Is. 41:21-29)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The idols could not save themselves, but God saves his people. (Is. 46:1-4) The folly of worshipping idols. (Is. 46:5-13)","God's judgments on Babylon. (Is. 47:1-6) Carelessness and confidence shall not prevent the evil. (Is. 47:7-15)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The welcome news of Christ's kingdom. (Is. 52:1-12) The humiliation of the Messiah. (Is. 52:13-15)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","An invitation to receive freely the blessings of the Saviour. (Is. 55:1-5) Gracious offers of pardon and peace. (Is. 55:6-13)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Christ's victory over his enemies. (Is. 63:1-6) His mercy toward his church. (Is. 63:7-14) The prayer of the church. (Is. 63:15-19)","The church prays that God's power may be manifested. (Is. 64:1-5) A confession of sin, and afflictions bewailed. (Is. 64:6-12)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":23,"name":"Jeremiah","chapters":52,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Jeremiah was a priest, a native of Anathoth, in the tribe of Benjamin. He was called to the prophetic office when very young, about seventy years after the death of Isaiah, and exercised it for about forty years with great faithfulness, till the sins of the Jewish nation came to their full measure and destruction followed. The prophecies of Jeremiah do not stand as they were delivered. Blayney has endeavoured to arrange them in more regular order, namely, Jer. 1-20; 22; 23; 25; 26; 35; 36; 45; 24; 29; 30; 31; 27; 28; 21; 34; 37; 32; 33; 38; 39:15-18, 1-14; 40-44; 46-52. The general subject of his prophecies is the idolatry and other sins of the Jews; the judgments by which they were threatened, with references to their future restoration and deliverance, and promises of the Messiah. They are remarkable for plain and faithful reproofs, affectionate expostulations, and awful warnings."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The absurdity of idolatry. (Jer. 10:1-16) Destruction denounced against Jerusalem. (Jer. 10:17-25)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","By the type of breaking an earthen vessel, Jeremiah is to predict the destruction of Judah","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Good and bad figs represent the Jews in captivity, and those who remain in their own land","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","A false prophet opposes Jeremiah. (Jer. 28:1-9) The false prophet warned of his approaching death. (Jer. 28:10-17)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The restoration of the Jews. (Jer. 33:1-13) The Messiah promised; happiness of his times. (Jer. 33:14-26)","Chapter explanation","The obedience of the Rechabites. (Jer. 35:1-11) The Jews' disobedience to the Lord. (Jer. 35:12-19)","Chapter explanation","The Chaldean army will return. (Jer. 37:1-10) Jeremiah is imprisoned. (Jer. 37:11-21)","Chapter explanation","The taking of Jerusalem. (Jer. 39:1-10) Jeremiah used well. (Jer. 39:11-14) Promises of safety to Ebed-melech. (Jer. 39:15-18)","Jeremiah is directed to go to Gedaliah. (Jer. 40:1-6) A conspiracy against Gedaliah. (Jer. 40:7-16)","Ishmael murders Gedaliah. (Jer. 41:1-10) Johanan recovers the captives, and purposes to retire to Egypt. (Jer. 41:11-18)","Chapter explanation","The leaders carry the people to Egypt. (Jer. 43:1-7) Jeremiah foretells the conquest of Egypt. (Jer. 43:8-13)","Chapter explanation","An encouragement sent to Baruch","Chapter explanation","The calamities of the Philistines","Prophecies against Moab for pride and security. (Jer. 48:1-13) For carnal confidence and contempt of God. (Jer. 48:14-47)","Chapter explanation","The ruin of Babylon. (Jer. 50:1-3,8-16,21-32,35-46;) The redemption of God's people. (Jer. 50:4-7,17-20,33,34)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":24,"name":"Lamentations","chapters":5,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["It is evident that Jeremiah was the author of the Lamentations which bear his name. The book was not written till after the destruction of Jerusalem by the Chaldeans. May we be led to consider sin as the cause of all our calamities, and under trials exercise submission, repentance, faith, and prayer, with the hope of promised deliverance through God's mercy."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","Lamentation for the misery of Jerusalem","The faithful lament their calamities, and hope in God's mercies","The deplorable state of the nation is contrasted with its ancient prosperity","The Jewish nation supplicating the Divine favour"]},{"id":25,"name":"Ezekiel","chapters":48,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Ezekiel was one of the priests; he was carried captive to Chaldea with Jehoiachin. All his prophecies appear to have been delivered in that country, at some place north of Babylon. Their chief object appears to have been to comfort his brethren in captivity. He is directed to warn of the dreadful calamities coming upon Judea, particularly upon the false prophets, and the neighbouring nations. Also to announce the future restoration of Israel and Judah from their several dispersions, and their happy state in their latter days, under the Messiah. Much of Christ will be found in this book, especially in the conclusion."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","The prophet is directed what he is to do. (Ezek. 2:1-5) And encouraged to be resolute, faithful, and devoted. (Ezek. 2:6-10)","Chapter explanation","The siege of Jerusalem. (Ezek. 4:1-8) The famine the inhabitants would suffer. (Ezek. 4:9-17)","A type of hair, showing the judgments about to come upon the Jews. (Ezek. 5:1-4) These awful judgments are declared. (Ezek. 5:5-17)","The Divine judgments for idolatry. (Ezek. 6:1-7) A remnant shall be saved. (Ezek. 6:8-10) The calamities are to be lamented. (Ezek. 6:11-14)","The desolation of the land. (Ezek. 7:1-15) The distress of the few who should escape. (Ezek. 7:16-22) The captivity. (Ezek. 7:23-27)","Chapter explanation","A vision denoting the destruction of the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and the departure of the symbol of the Divine presence","A vision of the burning of the city. (Ezek. 10:1-7) The Divine glory departing from the temple. (Ezek. 10:8-22)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Threatenings against hypocrites. (Ezek. 14:1-11) God's purpose to punish the guilty Jews, but a few should be saved. (Ezek. 14:12-23)","Jerusalem like an unfruitful vine","A parable showing the first low estate of the Jewish nation, its prosperity, idolatries, and punishment","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","A parable lamenting the ruin of Jehoahaz and Jehoiakim. (Ezek. 19:1-9) Another describing the desolation of the people. (Ezek. 19:10-14)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","A history of the apostacy of God's people from him, and the aggravation thereof","The fate of Jerusalem. (Ezek. 24:1-14) The extent of the sufferings of the Jews. (Ezek. 24:15-27)","Judgments against the Ammonites. (Ezek. 25:1-7) Against the Moabites, Edomites, and Philistines. (Ezek. 25:8-17)","A prophecy against Tyre","The merchandise of Tyre. (Ezek. 27:1-25) Its fall and ruin. (Ezek. 27:26-36)","Chapter explanation","The desolation of Egypt. (Ezek. 29:1-16) Also a promise of mercy to Israel. (Ezek. 29:17-21)","A prophecy against Egypt. (Ezek. 30:1-19) Another. (Ezek. 30:20-26)","The glory of Assyria. (Ezek. 31:1-9) Its fall, and the like for Egypt. (Ezek. 31:10-18)","The fall of Egypt. (Ezek. 32:1-16) It is like that of other nations. (Ezek. 32:17-32)","Chapter explanation","The rulers reproved. (Ezek. 34:1-6) The people are to be restored to their own land. (Ezek. 34:7-16) The kingdom of Christ. (Ezek. 34:17-31)","A prophecy against Edom","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","The army and malice of Gog. (Ezek. 38:1-13) God's judgments. (Ezek. 38:14-23)","The destruction of Gog. (Ezek. 39:1-10) Its extent. (Ezek. 39:11-22) Israel again favoured. (Ezek. 39:23-29)","The Vision of the Temple","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":26,"name":"Daniel","chapters":12,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Daniel was of noble birth, if not one of the royal family of Judah. He was carried captive to Babylon in the fourth year of Jehoiachin, B. C. 606, when a youth. He was there taught the learning of the Chaldeans, and held high offices, both under the Babylonian and Persian empires. He was persecuted for his religion, but was miraculously delivered; and lived to a great age, as he must have been about ninety-four years old at the time of the last of his visions. The book of Daniel is partly historical, relating various circumstances which befel himself and the Jews, at Babylon; but is chiefly prophetical, detailing visions and prophecies which foretell numerous important events relative to the four great empires of the world, the coming and death of the Messiah, the restoration of the Jews, and the conversion of the Gentiles. Though there are considerable difficulties in explaining the prophetical meaning of some passages in this book, we always find encouragement to faith and hope, examples worthy of imitation, and something to direct our thoughts to Christ Jesus upon the cross and on his glorious throne."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation","Daniel's vision of the four beasts. (Dan. 7:1-8) and of Christ's kingdom. (Dan. 7:9-14) The interpretation. (Dan. 7:15-28)","Daniel's vision of the ram and the he-goat. (Dan. 8:1-14) The interpretation of it. (Dan. 8:15-27)","Chapter explanation","Daniel's vision near the river Hiddekel. (Dan. 10:1-9) He is to expect a discovery of future events. (Dan. 10:10-21)","The vision of the Scriptures of truth","The conclusion of the vision of the Scriptures of truth. (Dan. 12:1-4) The times of the continuance of these events. (Dan. 12:5-13)"]},{"id":27,"name":"Hosea","chapters":14,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Hosea is supposed to have been of the kingdom of Israel. He lived and prophesied during a long period. The scope of his predictions appears to be, to detect, reprove, and convince the Jewish nation in general, and the Israelites in particular, of their many sins, particularly their idolatry: the corrupt state of the kingdom is also noticed. But he invites them to repentance, with promises of mercy, and gospel predictions of the future restoration of the Israelites and of the Jews, and their final conversion to Christianity."],"titles":["Chapter explanation","The idolatry of the people. (Hos. 2:1-5) God's judgments against them. (Hos. 2:6-13) His promises of reconciliation. (Hos. 2:14-23)","The prophet enters into a new contract, representing the gracious manner in which God will again restore Israel under a new covenant","Chapter explanation","The Divine judgments against Israel. (Hos. 5:1-7) Approaching desolations threatened. (Hos. 5:8-15)","An exhortation to repentance. (Hos. 6:1-3) Israel's instability and breach of the covenant. (Hos. 6:4-11)","The manifold sins of Israel. (Hos. 7:1-7) Their senselessness and hypocrisy. (Hos. 7:8-16)","Chapter explanation","The distress to come upon Israel. (Hos. 9:1-6) The approach of the day of trouble. (Hos. 9:7-10) Judgments on Israel. (Hos. 9:11-17)","The idolatry of Israel. (Hos. 10:1-8) They are exhorted to repentance. (Hos. 10:9-15)","God's regard for Israel; their ingratitude. (Hos. 11:1-7) The Divine mercy yet in store. (Hos. 11:8-12)","Judah and Israel reminded of the Divine favours. (Hos. 12:1-6) The provocations of Israel. (Hos. 12:7-14)","The abuse of God's favour leads to punishment. (Hos. 13:1-8) A promise of God's mercy. (Hos. 13:9-16)","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":28,"name":"Joel","chapters":3,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["From the desolations about to come upon the land of Judah, by the ravages of locusts and other insects, the prophet Joel exhorts the Jews to repentance, fasting, and prayer. He notices the blessings of the gospel, with the final glorious state of the church."],"titles":["A plague of locusts. (Joel 1:1-7) All sorts of people are called to lament it. (Joel 1:8-13) They are to look to God. (Joel 1:14-20)","Chapter explanation","Chapter explanation"]},{"id":29,"name":"Amos","chapters":9,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Amos was a herdsman, and engaged in agriculture. But the same Divine Spirit influenced Isaiah and Daniel in the court, and Amos in the sheep-folds, giving to each the powers and eloquence needful for them. He assures the twelve tribes of the destruction of the neighbouring nations; and as they at that time gave themselves up to wickedness and idolatry, he reproves the Jewish nation with severity; but describes the restoration of the church by the Messiah, extending to the latter days."],"titles":["Judgments against the Syrians, Philistines, Tyrians, Edomites, and Ammonites","Judgments against Moab and Judah · The ingratitude and ruin of Israel","Judgments against Israel · The like to other nations","Israel is reproved · Their impenitence shown","Israel is called to seek the Lord · Earnest exhortations to repentance","The danger of luxury and false security · Punishments of sins","Visions of judgments to come upon Israel · Amaziah threatens Amos","The near approach of the ruin of Israel · Oppression reproved","The ruin of Israel · The restoration of the Jews and the gospel blessing"]},{"id":30,"name":"Obadiah","chapters":1,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The first part denounces the destruction of Edom, dwelling upon the injuries they inflicted upon the Jews. The second foretells the restoration of the Jews, and the latter glories of the church."],"titles":["Destruction to come upon Edom. Their offences against Jacob · The restoration of the Jews, and their flourishing state in the latter times"]},{"id":31,"name":"Jonah","chapters":4,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Jonah was a native of Galilee, 2Ki 14:25. His miraculous deliverance from out of the fish, rendered him a type of our blessed Lord, who mentions it, so as to show the certain truth of the narrative. All that was done was easy to the almighty power of the Author and Sustainer of life. This book shows us, by the example of the Ninevites, how great are the Divine forbearance and long-suffering towards sinners. It shows a most striking contrast between the goodness and mercy of God, and the rebellion, impatience, and peevishness of his servant; and it will be best understood by those who are most acquainted with their own hearts."],"titles":["Jonah, sent to Nineveh, flees to Tarshish · He is stayed by a tempest","The prayer of Jonah · He is delivered from the fish","Jonah sent again to Nineveh, preaches there · Nineveh is spared upon the repentance of the inhabitants","Jonah repines at God's mercy to Nineveh, and is reproved · He is taught by the withering of a gourd, that he did wrong"]},{"id":32,"name":"Micah","chapters":7,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Micah was raised up to support Isaiah, and to confirm his predictions, while he invited to repentance, both by threatened judgments and promised mercies. A very remarkable passage, Mic 5 contains a summary of prophecies concerning the Messiah."],"titles":["The wrath of God against Israel · Also against Jerusalem and other cities, Their precautions vain","The sins and desolations of Israel · Their evil practices","The cruelty of the princes, and the falsehood of the prophets · Their false security","The peace of the kingdom of Christ · The judgments to come upon Jerusalem, but the final triumph of Israel","The birth of Christ and conversion of the Gentiles · The triumphs of Israel","God's controversy with Israel · The duties God requires","The general prevalence of wickedness · Reliance on God, and triumph over enemies"]},{"id":33,"name":"Nahum","chapters":3,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This prophet denounces the certain and approaching destruction of the Assyrian empire, particularly of Nineveh, which is described very minutely. Together with this is consolation for his countrymen, encouraging them to trust in God."],"titles":["The justice and power of the Lord · The overthrow of the Assyrians","Nineveh's destruction foretold · The true cause, their sinning against God, and his appearing against them","The sins and judgments of Nineveh · Its utter destruction"]},{"id":34,"name":"Habakkuk","chapters":3,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["The subject of this prophecy is the destruction of Judea and Jerusalem for the sins of the people, and the consolation of the faithful under national calamities."],"titles":["The wickedness of the land. The fearful vengeance to be executed · These judgments to be inflicted by a nation more wicked than themselves","Habakkuk must wait in faith · Judgments upon the Chaldeans","The prophet beseeches God for his people · He calls to mind former deliverances"]},{"id":35,"name":"Zephaniah","chapters":3,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Zephaniah excites to repentance, foretells the destruction of the enemies of the Jews, and comforts the pious among them with promises of future blessings, the restoration of their nation, and the prosperity of the church in the latter days."],"titles":["Threatenings against sinners · More threatenings","An exhortation to repentance · Judgments upon other nations","Further reproofs for sin · Encouragement to look for mercy"]},{"id":36,"name":"Haggai","chapters":2,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["After the return from captivity, Haggai was sent to encourage the people to rebuild the temple, and to reprove their neglect. To encourage their undertaking, the people are assured that the glory of the second temple shall far exceed that of the first, by the appearing therein of Christ, the Desire of all nations."],"titles":["Haggai reproves the Jews for neglecting the temple · He promises God's assistance to them","Greater glory promised to the second temple than to the first · Their sins hindered the work"]},{"id":37,"name":"Zechariah","chapters":14,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["This prophecy is suitable to all, as the scope is to reprove for sin, and threaten God's judgments against the impenitent, and to encourage those that feared God, with assurances of the mercy God had in store for his church, and especially of the coming of the Messiah, and the setting up his kingdom in the world."],"titles":["An exhortation to repentance · A vision of the ministry of angels","The prosperity of Jerusalem · The Jews called to return to their own land","The restoration of the church · A promise concerning the Messiah","A vision of a candlestick, with two olive trees · Further encouragement","The vision of a flying roll · The vision of a woman and an ephah","The vision of the chariots · Joshua, the high priest, crowned as a type of Christ","The captives' inquiry respecting fasting · Sin the cause of their captivity","The restoration of Jerusalem · The people encouraged by promises of God's favour, and exhorted to holiness","God's defence of his church · Christ's coming and his kingdom","Blessings to be sought from the Lord · God will restore his people","Destruction to come upon the Jews · The Lord's dealing with the Jews","Punishment of the enemies of Judah · Repentance and sorrow of the Jews","The Fountain for the remission of sins, The conviction of the false prophets · The death of Christ, and the saving of a remnant of the people","The sufferings of Jerusalem · Encouraging prospects, and the destruction of her enemies"]},{"id":38,"name":"Malachi","chapters":4,"testament":"Old Testament","intro":["Malachi was the last of the prophets, and is supposed to have prophesied B.C. 420. He reproves the priests and the people for the evil practices into which they had fallen, and invites them to repentance and reformation, with promises of the blessings to be bestowed at the coming of the Messiah. And now that prophecy was to cease, he speaks clearly of the Messiah, as nigh at hand, and directs the people of God to keep in rememberance the law of Moses, while they were in expectation of the gospel of Christ."],"titles":["The ingratitude of Israel · They are careless in God's institutions","The priests reproved for neglecting their covenant · The people reproved for their evil practices","The coming of Christ · The Jews reproved for their corruptions","The judgements on the wicked, and the happiness of the righteous · Regard to be had to the law; John the Baptist promised as the forerunner of Messiah"]},{"id":39,"name":"Matthew","chapters":28,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["Matthew, surnamed Levi, before his conversion was a publican, or tax-gatherer under the Romans at Capernaum. He is generally allowed to have written his Gospel before any other of the evangelists. The contents of this Gospel, and the evidence of ancient writers, show that it was written primarily for the use of the Jewish nation. The fulfilment of prophecy was regarded by the Jews as strong evidence, therefore this is especially dwelt upon by St. Matthew. Here are particularly selected such parts of our Saviour's history and discourses as were best suited to awaken the Jewish nation to a sense of their sins; to remove their erroneous expectations of an earthly kingdom; to abate their pride and self-conceit; to teach them the spiritual nature and extent of the gospel; and to prepare them for the admission of the Gentiles into the church."],"titles":["The genealogy of Jesus · An angel appears to Joseph","The wise men's search after Christ · The wise men worship Jesus","John the Baptist, His preaching, manner of life, and baptism · John reproves the Pharisees and Sadducees","The temptation of Christ · The opening of Christ's ministry in Galilee","Christ's sermon on the mount · Who are blessed","Against hypocrisy in almsgiving · Against hypocrisy in prayer","Christ reproves rash judgment · Encouragements to prayer","Multitudes follow Christ · He heals a leper","Jesus returns to Capernaum, and heals a paralytic · Matthew called","The apostles called · The apostles instructed and sent forth","Christ's preaching · Christ's answer to John's disciples","Jesus defends his disciples for plucking corn on the sabbath day · Jesus heals a man with a withered hand on the sabbath","The parable of the sower · The parable of the tares","Death of John the Baptist · Five thousand people miraculously fed","Jesus discourses about human traditions · He warns against things which really defile","The Pharisees and Sadducees ask a sign · Jesus cautions against the doctrine of the Pharisees","The transfiguration of Christ · Jesus casts out a dumb and deaf spirit","The importance of humility · Caution against offences","Jesus enters Judea · The Pharisees' question about divorces","The parable of the labourers in the vineyard · Jesus again foretells his sufferings","Christ enters Jerusalem · He drives out those who profaned the temple","The parable of the marriage feast · The Pharisees question Jesus as to the tribute","Jesus reproves the scribes and Pharisees · Crimes of the Pharisees","Christ foretells the destruction of the temple · The troubles before the destruction of Jerusalem","The parable of the ten virgins · The parable of the talents","The rulers conspire against Christ · Christ anointed at Bethany","Christ delivered to Pilate, The despair of Judas · Christ before Pilate","Christ's resurrection · He appears to the women"]},{"id":40,"name":"Mark","chapters":16,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["Mark was a sister's son to Barnabas, Col 4:10; and Ac 12:12 shows that he was the son of Mary, a pious woman of Jerusalem, at whose house the apostles and first Christians assembled. From Peter's styling him his son, 1Pe 5:13, the evangelist is supposed to have been converted by that apostle. Thus Mark was closely united with the followers of our Lord, if not himself one of the number. Mark wrote at Rome; some suppose that Peter dictated to him, though the general testimony is, that the apostle having preached at Rome, Mark, who was the apostle's companion, and had a clear understanding of what Peter delivered, was desired to commit the particulars to writing. And we may remark, that the great humility of Peter is very plain where any thing is said about himself. Scarcely an action or a work of Christ is mentioned, at which this apostle was not present, and the minuteness shows that the facts were related by an eye-witness. This Gospel records more of the miracles than of the discourses of our Lord, and though in many things it relates the same things as the Gospel according to St. Matthew, we may reap advantages from reviewing the same events, placed by each of the evangelists in that point of view which most affected his own mind."],"titles":["The office of John the Baptist · The baptism and temptation of Christ","Christ heals one sick of the palsy · Levi's call, and the entertainment given to Jesus","The withered hand healed · The people resort to Christ","The parable of the sower · Other parables","The demoniac healed · A woman healed","Christ despised in his own country · The apostles sent forth","The traditions of the elders · What defiles the man","Four thousand fed by a miracle · Christ cautions against the Pharisees and Herodians","The transfiguration · An evil spirit cast out","The Pharisees' question concerning divorce · Christ's love to little children","Christ's triumphant entry into Jerusalem · The barren fig-tree cursed, The temple cleansed","The parable of the vineyard and husbandmen · Question about tribute","The destruction of the temple foretold · Christ's prophetic declaration","Christ anointed at Bethany · The passover, Jesus declares that Judas would betray him","Christ before Pilate · Christ led to be crucified","Christ's resurrection made known the women · Christ appears to Mary Magdalene and other disciples"]},{"id":41,"name":"Luke","chapters":24,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This evangelist is generally supposed to have been a physician, and a companion of the apostle Paul. The style of his writings, and his acquaintance with the Jewish rites and usages, sufficiently show that he was a Jew, while his knowledge of the Greek language and his name, speak his Gentile origin. He is first mentioned Ac 16:10, 11, as with Paul at Troas, whence he attended him to Jerusalem, and was with him in his voyage, and in his imprisonment at Rome. This Gospel appears to be designed to supersede many defective and unauthentic narratives in circulation, and to give a genuine and inspired account of the life, miracles, and doctrines of our Lord, learned from those who heard and witnessed his discourses and miracles."],"titles":["The Preface · Zacharias and Elisabeth","The birth of Christ · It is made known to the shepherds","John the Baptist's ministry · John the Baptist testifies concerning Christ","The temptation of Christ · Christ in the synagogue of Nazareth","The miraculous draught of fishes, Peter, James, and John called · A leper cleansed","The disciples pluck corn on the sabbath · Works of mercy suitable to the sabbath day","The centurion's servant healed · The widow's son raised","The ministry of Christ · The parable of the sower","The apostles sent forth · The multitude miraculously fed","Seventy disciples sent forth · The blessedness of Christ's disciples","The disciples taught to pray · Christ encourages being earnest in prayer","Christ reproves the interpreters of the law · A caution against covetousness, The parable of the rich man","Christ exhorts to repentance from the case of the Galileans and others · Parable of the barren fig-tree","Christ heals a man on the sabbath · He teaches humility","Parables of the lost sheep, and the piece of silver · The prodigal son, his wickedness and distress","The parable of the unjust steward · Christ reproves the hypocrisy of the covetous Pharisees","To avoid offences, To pray for increase of faith, Humility taught · Ten lepers cleansed","The parable of the importunate widow · The Pharisee and the publican","The conversion of Zaccheus · The parable of the nobleman and his servants","The priests and scribes question Christ's authority · The parable of the vineyard and husbandmen","Christ commends a poor widow · His prophecy","The treachery of Judas · The passover","Christ before Pilate · Christ before Herod","The resurrection of Christ · He appears to two disciples on the way to Emmaus"]},{"id":42,"name":"John","chapters":21,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The apostle and evangelist, John, seems to have been the youngest of the twelve. He was especially favoured with our Lord's regard and confidence, so as to be spoken of as the disciple whom Jesus loved. He was very sincerely attached to his Master. He exercised his ministry at Jerusalem with much success, and outlived the destruction of that city, agreeably to Christ's prediction, ch. 21:22. History relates that after the death of Christ's mother, John resided chiefly at Ephesus. Towards the close of Domitian's reign he was banished to the isle of Patmos, where he wrote his Revelation. On the accession of Nerva, he was set at liberty, and returned to Ephesus, where it is thought he wrote his Gospel and Epistles, about A. D. 97, and died soon after. The design of this Gospel appears to be to convey to the Christian world, just notions of the real nature, office, and character of that Divine Teacher, who came to instruct and to redeem mankind. For this purpose, John was directed to select for his narrative, those passages of our Saviour's life, which most clearly displayed his Divine power and authority; and those of his discourses, in which he spake most plainly of his own nature, and of the power of his death, as an atonement for the sins of the world. By omitting, or only briefly mentioning, the events recorded by the other evangelists, John gave testimony that their narratives are true, and left room for the doctrinal statements already mentioned, and for particulars omitted in the other Gospels, many of which are exceedingly important."],"titles":["The Divinity of Christ · His Divine and human nature","The miracle at Cana · Christ casts the buyers and sellers out of the temple","Christ's discourse with Nicodemus · The baptism of John of Christ John's testimony","Christ's departure into Galilee · His discourse with the Samaritan woman","The cure at the pool of Bethesda · The Jews' displeasure","Five thousand miraculously fed · Jesus walks on the sea","Christ goes to the feast of tabernacles · His discourse at the feast","The Pharisees and the adulteress · Christ's discourse with the Pharisees","Christ give sight to one born blind · The account given by the blind man","The parable of the good shepherd · Christ the Door","The sickness of Lazarus · Christ returns to Judea","Christ anointed by Mary · He enters Jerusalem","Christ washes the disciples' feet · The treachery of Judas foretold","Christ comforts his disciples · He further comforts his disciples","Christ the true Vine · His love to his disciples","Persecution foretold · The promise of the Holy Spirit, and his office","Christ's prayer for himself · His prayer for his disciples","Christ taken in the garden · Christ before Annas and Caiaphas","Christ condemned and crucified · Christ on the cross","The sepulchre found to be empty · Christ appears to Mary","Christ appears to his disciples · His discourse with Peter"]},{"id":43,"name":"Acts","chapters":28,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This book unites the Gospels to the Epistles. It contains many particulars concerning the apostles Peter and Paul, and of the Christian church from the ascension of our Saviour to the arrival of St. Paul at Rome, a space of about thirty years. St. Luke was the writer of this book; he was present at many of the events he relates, and attended Paul to Rome. But the narrative does not afford a complete history of the church during the time to which it refers, nor even of St. Paul's life. The object of the book has been considered to be, 1. To relate in what manner the gifts of the Holy Spirit were communicated on the day of Pentecost, and the miracles performed by the apostles, to confirm the truth of Christianity, as showing that Christ's declarations were really fulfilled. 2. To prove the claim of the Gentiles to be admitted into the church of Christ. This is shown by much of the contents of the book. A large portion of the Acts is occupied by the discourses or sermons of various persons, the language and manner of which differ, and all of which will be found according to the persons by whom they were delivered, and the occasions on which they were spoken. It seems that most of these discourses are only the substance of what was actually delivered. They relate nevertheless fully to Jesus as the Christ, the anointed Messiah."],"titles":["Proofs of Christ's resurrection · Christ's ascension","The descent of the Holy Spirit at the day of Pentecost · The apostles speak in divers languages","A lame man healed by Peter and John · Peter's address to the Jews","Peter and John imprisoned · The apostles boldly testify to Christ","The death of Ananias and Sapphira · The power which accompanied the preaching of the gospel","The appointment of deacons · Stephen falsely accused of blasphemy","Stephen's defence · Stephen reproves the Jews for the death of Christ","Saul persecutes the church · Philip's success at Samaria. Simon the sorcerer baptized","The conversion of Saul · Saul converted preaches Christ","Cornelius directed to send for Peter · Peter's vision","Peter's defence · The success of the gospel at Antioch","The martyrdom of James, and the imprisonment of Peter · He is delivered from prison by an angel","The mission of Paul and Barnabas · Elymas the sorcerer","Paul and Barnabas at Iconium · A cripple healed at Lystra, The people would have sacrificed to Paul and Barnabas","The dispute raised by Judaizing teachers · The council at Jerusalem","Paul takes Timothy to be his assistant · Paul proceeds to Macedonia, The conversion of Lydia","Paul at Thessalonica · The noble conduct of the Bereans","Paul at Corinth, with Aquila and Priscilla · He continues to preach at Corinth","Paul instructs the disciples of John at Ephesus · He teaches there","Paul's journeys · Eutychus restored to life","Paul's voyage towards Jerusalem · Paul at Cesarea. The prophecy of Agabus, Paul at Jerusalem","Paul's account of his conversion · Paul directed to preach to the Gentiles","Paul's defence before the council of the Jews · Paul's defence. He receives a Divine assurance that he shall go to Rome","The speech of Tertullus against Paul · Paul's defence before Felix","Paul before Festus, he appeals to Caesar · Festus confers with Agrippa respecting Paul","Paul's defence before Agrippa · His conversion and preaching to the Gentiles","Paul's voyage towards Rome · Paul and his companions endangered by a tempest","Paul kindly received at Melita · He arrives at Rome"]},{"id":44,"name":"Romans","chapters":16,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The scope or design of the apostle in writing to the Romans appears to have been, to answer the unbelieving, and to teach the believing Jew; to confirm the Christian and to convert the idolatrous Gentile; and to show the Gentile convert as equal with the Jewish, in respect of his religious condition, and his rank in the Divine favour. These several designs are brought into on view, by opposing or arguing with the infidel or unbelieving Jew, in favour of the Christian or believing Gentile. The way of a sinner's acceptance with God, or justification in his sight, merely by grace, through faith in the righteousness of Christ, without distinction of nations, is plainly stated. This doctrine is cleared from the objections raised by Judaizing Christians, who were for making terms of acceptance with God by a mixture of the law and the gospel, and for shutting out the Gentiles from any share in the blessings of salvation brought in by the Messiah. In the conclusion, holiness is further enforced by practical exhortations."],"titles":["The apostle's commission · Prays for the saints at Rome, and expresses his desire to see them","The Jews could not be justified by the law of Moses, any more than the Gentiles by the law of nature · The sins of the Jews confuted all their vain confidence in their outward privileges","Objections answered · All mankind are sinners","The doctrine of justification by faith is shown by the case of Abraham · He received the promise through the righteousness of faith","The happy effects of justification through faith in the righteousness of Christ · That we are reconciled by his blood","Believers must die to sin, and live to God · This is urged by their Christian baptism and union with Christ","Believers are united to Christ, that they may bring forth fruit unto God · The use and excellence of the law","The freedom of believers from condemnation · Their privileges as being the children of God","The apostle's concern that his countrymen were strangers to the gospel · The promises are made good to the spiritual seed of Abraham","The apostle's earnest desire for the salvation of the Jews · The difference between the righteousness of the law, and the righteousness of faith","The rejection of the Jews is not universal · God overruled their unbelief for making the Gentiles partakers of gospel privileges","Believers are to dedicate themselves to God · To be humble, and faithfully to use their spiritual gifts, in their respective stations","The duty of subjection to governors · Exhortations to mutual love","The Jewish converts cautioned against judging, and Gentile believers against despising one the other · And the Gentiles exhorted to take heed of giving offence in their use of indifferent things","Directions how to behave towards the weak · All to receive one another as brethren","The apostle recommends Phebe to the church at Rome, and greets several friends there · Cautions the church against such as made divisions"]},{"id":45,"name":"1 Corinthians","chapters":16,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The Corinthian church contained some Jews, but more Gentiles, and the apostle had to contend with the superstition of the one, and the sinful conduct of the other. The peace of this church was disturbed by false teachers, who undermined the influence of the apostle. Two parties were the result; one contending earnestly for the Jewish ceremonies, the other indulging in excesses contrary to the gospel, to which they were especially led by the luxury and the sins which prevailed around them. This epistle was written to rebuke some disorderly conduct, of which the apostle had been apprized, and to give advice as to some points whereon his judgment was requested by the Corinthians. Thus the scope was twofold. 1. To apply suitable remedies to the disorders and abuses which prevailed among them. 2. To give satisfactory answers on all the points upon which his advice had been desired. The address, and Christian mildness, yet firmness, with which the apostle writes, and goes on from general truths directly to oppose the errors and evil conduct of the Corinthians, is very remarkable. He states the truth and the will of God, as to various matters, with great force of argument and animation of style."],"titles":["A salutation and thanksgiving · Exhortation to brotherly love, and reproof for divisions","The plain manner in which the apostle preached Christ crucified · The wisdom contained in this doctrine","The Corinthians reproved for their contentions · The true servants of Christ can do nothing without him","The true character of gospel ministers · Cautions against despising the apostle","The apostle blames the Corinthians for connivance at an incestuous person; · and directs their behaviour towards those guilty of scandalous crimes","Cautions against going to law in heathen courts · Sins which, if lived and died in, shut out from the kingdom of God","The apostle answers several questions about marriage · Married Christians should not seek to part from their unbelieving consorts","The danger of having a high conceit of knowledge · The mischief of offending weak brethren","The apostle shows his authority, and asserts his right to be maintained · He waved this part of his Christian liberty, for the good of others","The great privileges, and yet terrible overthrow of the Israelites in the wilderness · Cautions against all idolatrous, and other sinful practices","The apostle, after an exhortation to follow him, · corrects some abuses","The variety of use of spiritual gifts are shown · In the human body every member has its place and use","The necessity and advantage of the grace of love · Its excellency represented by its properties and effects;","Prophecy preferred to the gift of tongues · The unprofitableness of speaking in unknown languages","The apostle proves the resurrection of Christ from the dead · Those answered who deny the resurrection of the body","A collection for the poor at Jerusalem · Timothy and Apollos commended"]},{"id":46,"name":"2 Corinthians","chapters":13,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The second epistle to the Corinthians probably was written about a year after the first. Its contents are closely connected with those of the former epistle. The manner in which the letter St. Paul formerly wrote had been received, is particularly noticed; this was such as to fill his heart with gratitude to God, who enabled him fully to discharge his duty towards them. Many had shown marks of repentance, and amended their conduct, but others still followed their false teachers; and as the apostle delayed his visit, from his unwillingness to treat them with severity, they charged him with levity and change of conduct. Also, with pride, vain-glory, and severity, and they spake of him with contempt. In this epistle we find the same ardent affection towards the disciples at Corinth, as in the former, the same zeal for the honour of the gospel, and the same boldness in giving Christian reproof. The first six chapters are chiefly practical: the rest have more reference to the state of the Corinthian church, but they contain many rules of general application."],"titles":["The apostle blesses God for comfort in, and deliverance out of troubles · He professes his own and his fellow-labourers' integrity","Reasons for the apostle not coming to Corinth · Directions about restoring the repentant offender","The preference of the gospel to the law given by Moses · The preaching of the apostle was suitable to the excellency and evidence of the gospel, through the power of the Holy Ghost","The apostles laboured with much diligence, sincerity, and faithfulness · Their sufferings for the gospel were great, yet with rich supports","The apostle's hope and desire of heavenly glory · This excited to diligence. The reasons of his being affected with zeal for the Corinthians","The apostle, with others, proved themselves faithful ministers of Christ, by their unblamable life and behaviour · By affection for them, And by earnest concern, that they might have no fellowship with unbelievers and idolaters","An exhortation to holiness, and the whole church entreated to bear affection to the apostle · He rejoiced in their sorrowing to repentance","The apostle reminds them of charitable contributions for the poor saints · Enforces this by their gifts, and by the love and grace of Christ","The reason for sending Titus to collect their alms · The Corinthians to be liberal and cheerful, The apostle thanks God for his unspeakable gift","The apostle states his authority with meekness and humility · Reasons with the Corinthians","The apostle gives the reasons for speaking in his own commendation · Shows that he had freely preached the gospel","The apostle's revelations · Which were improved to his spiritual advantage","The apostle threatens obstinate offenders · He prays for their reformation"]},{"id":47,"name":"Galatians","chapters":6,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The churches in Galatia were formed partly of converted Jews, and partly of Gentile converts, as was generally the case. St. Paul asserts his apostolic character and the doctrines he taught, that he might confirm the Galatian churches in the faith of Christ, especially with respect to the important point of justification by faith alone. Thus the subject is mainly the same as that which is discussed in the epistle to the Romans, that is, justification by faith alone. In this epistle, however, attention is particularly directed to the point, that men are justified by faith without the works of the law of Moses. Of the importance of the doctrines prominently set forth in this epistle, Luther thus speaks: “We have to fear as the greatest and nearest danger, lest Satan take from us this doctrine of faith, and bring into the church again the doctrine of works and of men's traditions. Wherefore it is very necessary that this doctrine be kept in continual practice and public exercise, both of reading and hearing. If this doctrine be lost, then is also the doctrine of truth, life and salvation, lost and gone.”"],"titles":["The apostle Paul asserts his apostolic character against such as lessened it · He reproves the Galatians for revolting from the gospel of Christ under the influence of evil teachers","The apostle declares his being owned as an apostle of the Gentiles · He had publicly opposed Peter for judaizing","The Galatians reproved for departing from the great doctrine of justification alone, through faith in Christ · This doctrine established from the example of Abraham","The folly of returning to legal observances for justification · The happy change made in the Gentile believers","An earnest exhortation to stand fast in the liberty of the gospel · To take heed of indulging a sinful temper","Exhortations to meekness, gentleness, and humility · To kindness towards all men, especially believers"]},{"id":48,"name":"Ephesians","chapters":6,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle was written when St. Paul was a prisoner at Rome. The design appears to be to strengthen the Ephesians in the faith of Christ, and to give exalted views of the love of God, and of the dignity and excellence of Christ, fortifying their minds against the scandal of the cross. He shows that they were saved by grace, and that however wretched they once were, they now had equal privileges with the Jews. He encourages them to persevere in their Christian calling, and urges them to walk in a manner becoming their profession, faithfully discharging the general and common duties of religion, and the special duties of particular relations."],"titles":["A salutation, and an account of saving blessings, as prepared in God's eternal election, as purchased by Christ's blood · And as conveyed in effectual calling: this is applied to the believing Jews, and to the believing Gentiles","The riches of God's grace towards men, shown from their deplorable state by nature, and the happy change Divine grace makes in them · The Ephesians called to reflect on their state of heathenism","The apostle sets forth his office, and his qualifications for it, and his call to it · Also the noble purposes answered by it","Exhortations to mutual forbearance and union · To a due use of spiritual gifts and graces","Exhortation to brotherly love · Cautions against several sins","The duties of children and parents · Of servants and masters"]},{"id":49,"name":"Philippians","chapters":4,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The Philippians felt a very deep interest for the apostle. The scope of the epistle is to confirm them in the faith, to encourage them to walk as becomes the gospel of Christ, to caution them against judaizing teachers, and to express gratitude for their Christian bounty. This epistle is the only one, among those written by St. Paul, in which no censures are implied or expressed. Full commendation and confidence are in every part, and the Philippians are addressed with a peculiar affection, which every serious reader will perceive."],"titles":["The apostle offers up thanksgivings and prayers, for the good work of grace in the Philippians · He expresses affection, and prays for them","Exhortations to a kind, humble spirit and behaviour · The example of Christ","The apostle cautions the Philippians against judaizing false teachers, and renounces his own former privileges · Expresses earnest desire to be found in Christ; also his pressing on toward perfection; and recommends his own example to other believers","The apostle exhorts the Philippians to stand fast in the Lord · Gives directions to some, and to all in general"]},{"id":50,"name":"Colossians","chapters":4,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle was sent because of some difficulties which arose among the Colossians, probably from false teachers, in consequence of which they sent to the apostle. The scope of the epistle is to show, that all hope of man's redemption is founded on Christ, in whom alone are all complete fulness, perfections, and sufficiency. The Colossians are cautioned against the devices of judaizing teachers, and also against the notions of carnal wisdom, and human inventions and traditions, as not consistent with full reliance on Christ. In the first two chapters the apostle tells them what they must believe, and in the two last what they must do; the doctrine of faith, and the precepts of life for salvation."],"titles":["The apostle Paul salutes the Colossians, and blesses God for their faith, love, and hope · Prays for their fruitfulness in spiritual knowledge","The apostle expresses his love to, and joy in believers · He cautions against the errors of heathen philosophy; also against Jewish traditions, and rites which had been fulfilled in Christ","The Colossians exhorted to be heavenly-minded; · to mortify all corrupt affections;","Masters to do their duty towards servants · Persons of all ranks to persevere in prayer, and Christian prudence"]},{"id":51,"name":"1 Thessalonians","chapters":5,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle is generally considered to have been the first of those written by St. Paul. The occasion seems to have been the good report of the stedfastness of the church at Thessalonica in the faith of the gospel. It is full of affection and confidence, and more consolatory and practical, and less doctrinal, than some of the other epistles."],"titles":["The faith, love, and patience of the Thessalonians, are evident tokens of their election which was manifested in the power with which the gospel came to them · Its powerful and exemplary effects upon their hearts and lives","The apostle reminds the Thessalonians of his preaching and behaviour · And of their receiving the gospel as the word of God","The apostle sent Timothy to establish and comfort the Thessalonians · He rejoiced at the good tidings of their faith and love","Exhortations to purity and holiness · To brotherly love, peaceable behaviour, and diligence","The apostle exhorts to be always ready for the coming of Christ to judgment, which will be with suddenness and surprise · He directs to several particular duties"]},{"id":52,"name":"2 Thessalonians","chapters":3,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The second epistle to the Thessalonians was written soon after the first. The apostle was told that, from some expressions in his first letter, many expected the second coming of Christ was at hand, and that the day of judgment would arrive in their time. Some of these neglected their worldly duties. St. Paul wrote again to correct their error, which hindered the spread of the gospel. He had written agreeably to the words of the prophets of the Old Testament; and he tells them there were many counsels of the Most High yet to be fulfilled, before that day of the Lord should come, though, because it is sure, he had spoken of it as near. The subject led to a remarkable foretelling, of some of the future events which were to take place in the after-ages of the Christian church, and which show the prophetic spirit the apostle possessed."],"titles":["The apostle blesses God for the growing state of the love and patience of the Thessalonians · And encourages them to persevere under all their sufferings for Christ, considering his coming at the great day of account","Cautions against the error that the time of Christ's coming was just at hand. There would first be a general apostasy from the faith, and a revealing of the antichristian man of sin · His destruction, and that of those who obey him","The apostle expresses confidence in the Thessalonians, and prays for them · He charges them to withdraw from disorderly walkers, particularly from the lazy and busybodies"]},{"id":53,"name":"1 Timothy","chapters":6,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The design of the epistle appears to be, that Timothy having been left at Ephesus, St. Paul wrote to instruct him in the choice of proper officers in the church, as well as in the exercise of a regular ministry. Also, to caution against the influence of false teachers, who by subtle distinctions and endless disputes, corrupted the purity and simplicity of the gospel. He presses upon him constant regard to the greatest diligence, faithfulness, and zeal. These subjects occupy the first four chapters; the fifth chapter instructs respecting particular classes; in the latter part, controversies and disputes are condemned, the love of money blamed, and the rich exhorted to good works."],"titles":["The apostle salutes Timothy · The design of the law as given by Moses","Prayer to be made for all persons, since the grace of the gospel makes no difference of ranks or stations · How men and women ought to behave, both in their religious and common life","The qualifications and behaviour of gospel bishops · And of deacons and their wives","Of departures from the faith that began already to appear · Several directions, with motives for due discharge of duties","Directions as to the elder and younger men and women · And as to poor widows","The duty of Christians towards believing, as well as other masters · The advantage of godliness with contentment"]},{"id":54,"name":"2 Timothy","chapters":4,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The first design of this epistle seems to have been, to apprize Timothy of what had occurred during the imprisonment of the apostle, and to request him to come to Rome. But being uncertain whether he should be suffered to live to see him, Paul gives a variety of advices and encouragements, for the faithful discharge of his ministerial duties. As this was a private epistle written to St. Paul's most intimate friend, under the miseries of imprisonment, and in the near prospect of death, it shows the temper and character of the apostle, and contains convincing proofs that he sincerely believed the doctrines he preached."],"titles":["Paul expresses great affection for Timothy · Exhorts him to improve his spiritual gifts","The apostle exhorts Timothy to persevere with diligence, like a soldier, a combatant, and a husbandman · Encouraging him by assurances of a happy end of his faithfulness","The apostle foretells the rise of dangerous enemies to the gospel · Proposes his own example to Timothy","The apostle solemnly charges Timothy to be diligent, though many will not bear sound doctrine · Enforces the charge from his own martyrdom, then at hand"]},{"id":55,"name":"Titus","chapters":3,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle chiefly contains directions to Titus concerning the elders of the Church, and the manner in which he should give instruction; and the latter part tells him to urge obedience to magistrates, to enforce good works, avoid foolish questions, and shun heresies. The instructions the apostle gave are all plain and simple. The Christian religion was not formed to answer worldly or selfish views, but it is the wisdom of God and the power of God."],"titles":["The apostle salutes Titus · The qualifications of a faithful pastor","The duties which become sound doctrine · Believing servants must be obedient","Obedience to magistrates, and becoming behaviour towards all, are enforced from what believers were before conversion, and what they are made, through Christ · Good works to be done, and useless disputes avoided"]},{"id":56,"name":"Philemon","chapters":1,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["Philemon was an inhabitant of Colosse, a person of some note and wealth, and a convert under the ministry of St. Paul. Onesimus was the slave of Philemon: having run away from his master, he went to Rome, where he was converted to the Christian faith, by the word as set forth by Paul, who kept him till his conduct proved the truth and sincerity of his conversion. He wished to repair the injury he had done to his master, but fearing the punishment his offence deserved might be inflicted, he entreated the apostle to write to Philemon. And St. Paul seems no where to reason more beautifully, or to entreat more forcibly, than in this epistle."],"titles":["The apostle's joy and praise for Philemon's steady faith in the Lord Jesus, and love to all the saints · He recommends Onesimus as one who would make rich amends for the misconduct of which he had been guilty; and on behalf of whom the apostle promises to make up any loss Philemon had sustained"]},{"id":57,"name":"Hebrews","chapters":13,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle shows Christ as the end, foundation, body, and truth of the figures of the law, which of themselves were no virtue for the soul. The great truth set forth in this epistle is that Jesus of Nazareth is the true God. The unconverted Jews used many arguments to draw their converted brethren from the Christian faith. They represented the law of Moses as superior to the Christian dispensation, and spoke against every thing connected with the Saviour. The apostle, therefore, shows the superiority of Jesus of Nazareth, as the Son of God, and the benefits from his sufferings and death as the sacrifice for sin, so that the Christian religion is much more excellent and perfect than that of Moses. And the principal design seems to be, to bring the converted Hebrews forward in the knowledge of the gospel, and thus to establish them in the Christian faith, and to prevent their turning from it, against which they are earnestly warned. But while it contains many things suitable to the Hebrews of early times, it also contains many which can never cease to interest the church of God; for the knowledge of Jesus Christ is the very marrow and kernel of all the Scriptures. The ceremonial law is full of Christ, and all the gospel is full of Christ; the blessed lines of both Testaments meet in Him; and how they both agree and sweetly unite in Jesus Christ, is the chief object of the epistle to the Hebrews to discover."],"titles":["The surpassing dignity of the Son of God in his Divine person, and in his creating and mediatorial work · And in his superiority to all the holy angels","The duty of stedfastly adhering to Christ and his gospel · His sufferings are no objection against his pre-eminence","The superior worth and dignity of Christ above Moses is shown · The Hebrews are warned of the sin and danger of unbelief","Humble, cautious fear is urged, lest any should come short of the promised rest, through unbelief · Arguments and motives to faith and hope in our approaches to God","The office and duty of a high priest abundantly answered in Christ · The Christian Hebrews reproved for their little progress in the knowledge of the gospel","The Hebrews are urged to go forward in the doctrine of Christ, and the consequences of apostacy, or turning back, are described · The apostle expresses satisfaction, as to the most of them","A comparison between the priesthood of Melchizedec and that of Christ · The excellence of Christ's priesthood above the Levitical priesthood is shown","The excellence of Christ's priesthood above that of Aaron is shown · The great excellence of the new covenant above the former","The Jewish tabernacle and its utensils · Their use and meaning","The insufficiency of sacrifices for taking away sin, The necessity and power of the sacrifice of Christ for that purpose · An argument for holy boldness in the believer's access to God through Jesus Christ, And for steadfastness in faith, and mutual love and duty","The nature and power of faith described · It is set forth by instances from Abel to Noah","An exhortation to be constant and persevere, The example of Christ is set forth, and the gracious design of God in all the sufferings believers endured · Peace and holiness are recommended, with cautions against despising spiritual blessings","Exhortations to various duties, and to be content with what Providence allots · To respect the instructions of faithful pastors, with cautions against being carried away by strange doctrines"]},{"id":58,"name":"James","chapters":5,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle of James is one of the most instructive writings in the New Testament. Being chiefly directed against particular errors at that time brought in among the Jewish Christians, it does not contain the same full doctrinal statements as the other epistles, but it presents an admirable summary of the practical duties of all believers. The leading truths of Christianity are set forth throughout; and on attentive consideration, it will be found entirely to agree with St. Paul's statements concerning grace and justification, while it abounds with earnest exhortations to the patience of hope and obedience of faith and love, interspersed with warnings, reproofs, and encouragements, according to the characters addressed. The truths laid down are very serious, and necessary to be maintained; and the rules for practice ought to be observed in all times. In Christ there are no dead and sapless branches, faith is not an idle grace; wherever it is, it brings forth fruit in works."],"titles":["How to apply to God under troubles, and how to behave in prosperous and in adverse circumstances · To look upon all evil as proceeding from ourselves, and all good from God","All professions of faith are vain, if not producing love and justice to others · The necessity of good works to prove the sincerity of faith, which otherwise will be of no more advantage than the faith of devils","Cautions against proud behaviour, and the mischief of an unruly tongue · The excellence of heavenly wisdom, in opposition to that which is worldly","Here are cautions against corrupt affections, and love of this world, which is enmity to God · Exhortations to undertake no affairs of life, without constant regard to the will and providence of God","The judgments of God denounced against rich unbelievers · Exhortation to patience and meekness under tribulations"]},{"id":59,"name":"1 Peter","chapters":5,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The same great doctrines, as in St. Paul's epistles, are here applied to same practical purposes. And this epistle is remarkable for the sweetness, gentleness, and humble love, with which it is written. It gives a short, and yet a very clear summary, both of the consolations and the instructions needful for the encouragement and direction of a Christian in his journey to heaven, raising his thoughts and desires to that happiness, and strengthening him against all opposition in the way, both from corruption within, and temptations and afflictions without."],"titles":["The apostle blesses God for his special benefits through Christ · Salvation by Christ foretold in ancient prophecy","A temper suitable to the Christian character as born again, is recommended · Holy conversation among the Gentiles directed","The duties of wives and husbands · Christians exhorted to agree","The consideration of Christ's sufferings is urged for purity and holiness · And the approaching end of the Jewish state, as a reason for sobriety, watchfulness, and prayer","Elders exhorted and encouraged · Younger Christians are to submit to their elders, and to yield with humility and patience to God, and to be sober, watchful, and stedfast in faith"]},{"id":60,"name":"2 Peter","chapters":3,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle clearly is connected with the former epistle of Peter. The apostle having stated the blessings to which God has called Christians, exhorts those who had received these precious gifts, to endeavour to improve in graces and virtues. They are urged to this from the wickedness of false teachers. They are guarded against impostors and scoffers, by disproving their false assertions, ch. 3:1–7, and by showing why the great day of Christ's coming was delayed, with a description of its awful circumstances and consequences; and suitable exhortations to diligence and holiness are given."],"titles":["Exhortations to add the exercise of various other graces to faith · The apostle looks forward to his approaching decease","Believers are cautioned against false teachers, and the certainty of their punishment shown from examples · An account of these seducers, as exceedingly wicked","The design here is to remind of Christ's final coming to judgement · He will appear unexpectedly, when the present frame of nature will be dissolved by fire"]},{"id":61,"name":"1 John","chapters":5,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle is a discourse upon the principles of Christianity, in doctrine and practice. The design appears to be, to refute and guard against erroneous and unholy tenets, principles, and practices, especially such as would lower the Godhead of Christ, and the reality and power of his sufferings and death, as an atoning sacrifice; and against the assertion that believers being saved by grace, are not required to obey the commandments. This epistle also stirs up all who profess to know God, to have communion with him, and to believe in him, and that they walk in holiness, not in sin, showing that a mere outward profession is nothing, without the evidence of a holy life and conduct. It also helps forward and excites real Christians to communion with God and the Lord Jesus Christ, to constancy in the true faith, and to purity of life."],"titles":["The apostle prefaces his epistle to believers in general, with evident testimonies to Christ, for promoting their happiness and joy · The necessity of a life of holiness, in order to communion with God, is shown","The apostle directs to the atonement of Christ for help against sinful infirmities · The effects of saving knowledge in producing obedience, and love to the brethren","The apostle admires the love of God in making believers his children · The purifying influence of the hope of seeing Christ, and the danger of pretending to this, and living in sin","Believers cautioned against giving heed to every one that pretends to the Spirit · Brotherly love enforced","Brotherly love is the effect of the new birth, which makes obedience to all God's commandments pleasant · Reference to witnesses agreeing to prove that Jesus, the Son of God, is the true Messiah"]},{"id":62,"name":"2 John","chapters":1,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle is like an abridgement of the first; it touches, in few words, on the same points. The Lady Electa is commended for her virtuous and religious education of her children; is exhorted to abide in the doctrine of Christ, to persevere in the truth, and carefully to avoid the delusions of false teachers. But chiefly the apostle beseeches her to practise the great commandment of Christian love and charity."],"titles":["The apostle salutes the elect lady and her children · Express his joy in their faith and love"]},{"id":63,"name":"3 John","chapters":1,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle is addressed to a converted Gentile. The scope is to commend his stedfastness in the faith, and his hospitality, especially to the ministers of Christ."],"titles":["The apostle commends Gaius for piety and hospitality · Cautions him against siding with Diotrephes, who was a turbulent spirit; but recommends Demetrius as a man of excellent character"]},{"id":64,"name":"Jude","chapters":1,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["This epistle is addressed to all believers in the gospel. Its design appears to be to guard believers against the false teachers who had begun to creep into the Christian church, and to scatter dangerous tenets, by attempting to lower all Christianity into a merely nominal belief and outward profession of the gospel. Having thus denied the obligations of personal holiness, they taught their disciples to live in sinful courses, at the same time flattering them with the hope of eternal life. The vile character of these seducers is shown, and their sentence is denounced, and the epistle concludes with warnings, admonitions, and counsels to believers."],"titles":["The apostle exhorts to stedfastness in the faith · The danger of being infected by false professors, and the dreadful punishment which shall be inflicted on them and their followers"]},{"id":65,"name":"Revelation","chapters":22,"testament":"New Testament","intro":["The Book of the Revelation of St. John consists of two principal divisions. 1. Relates to “the things which are,” that is, the then present state of the church, and contains the epistle of John to the seven churches, and his account of the appearance of the Lord Jesus, and his direction to the apostle to write what he beheld, ch. 1:9–20. Also the addresses or epistles to seven churches of Asia. These, doubtless, had reference to the state of the respective churches, as they then existed, but contain excellent precepts and exhortations, commendations and reproofs, promises and threatenings, suitable to instruct the Christian church at all times. 2. Contains a prophecy of “the things which shall be hereafter,” and describes the future state of the church, from the time when the apostle beheld the visions here recorded. It is intended for our spiritual improvement; to warn the careless sinner, point out the way of salvation to the awakened inquirer, build up the weak believer, comfort the afflicted and tempted Christian, and, we may especially add, to strengthen the martyr of Christ, under the cruel persecutions and sufferings inflicted by Satan and his followers."],"titles":["The Divine origin, the design, and the importance of this book · The apostle John salutes the seven churches of Asia","Epistles to the churches in Asia, with warnings and encouragements, To the church at Ephesus; · at Smyrna;","Epistles to the church at Sardis; · at Philadelphia;","A vision of God, as on his glorious throne, around which were twenty-four elders and four living creatures · Whose songs, and those of the holy angels, the apostle heard","A book sealed with seven seals, which could be opened by none but Christ, who took the book to open it · Upon which all honour is ascribed to him, as worthy to open it","The opening of the seals, The first, second, third, and fourth · The fifth","A pause between two great periods · The peace, happiness, and safety of the saints, as signified by an angel's sealing 144,000","The seventh seal is opened and seven angels appear with seven trumpets, ready to proclaim the purposes of God · Another angel casts fire on the earth, which produces terrible storms of vengeance","The fifth trumpet is followed by a representation of another star as falling from heaven and opening the bottomless pit, out of which come swarms of locusts · The sixth trumpet is followed by the loosing of four angels bound in the great river Euphrates","The Angel of the covenant presents a little open book, which is followed with seven thunders · At the end of the following prophecies, time should be no more","The state of the church is represented under the figure of a temple measured · Two witnesses prophesy is sackcloth","A description of the church of Christ and of Satan, under the figures of a woman and of a great red dragon · Michael and his angels fight against the devil and his angels, who are defeated","A wild beast rises out of the sea, to whom the dragon gives his power · Another beast, which has two horns like a lamb, but speaks as a dragon","Those faithful to Christ celebrate the praises of God · Three angels; one proclaiming the everlasting gospel; another, the downfal of Babylon; and a third, the dreadful wrath of God on the worshippers of the beast. The blessedness of those who die in the Lord","A song of praise is sung by the church · Seven angels with the seven plagues; and to them one of the living creatures gives seven golden vials full of the wrath of God","The first vial is poured out on the earth, the second on the sea, the third on the rivers and fountains · The fourth on the sun, the fifth on the seat of the beast","One of the angels who had the vials, explains the meaning of the former vision of the antichristian beast that was to reign 1260 years, and then to be destroyed · And interprets the mystery of the woman, and the beast that had seven heads and ten horns","Another angel from heaven proclaims the fall of mystical Babylon · A voice from heaven admonishes the people of God, lest they partake of her plagues","The church in heaven and that on earth triumph, and praise the Lord for his righteous judgments · A vision of Christ going forth to destroy the beast and his armies","Satan is bound for a thousand years · The first resurrection; those are blessed that have part therein","A new heaven, and new earth: the new Jerusalem where God dwells, and banishes all sorrow from his people · Its heavenly origin, glory, and secure defence","A description of the heavenly state, under the figures of the water and the tree of life, and of the throne of God and the Lamb · The truth and certain fulfilling of all the prophetic visions, The Holy Spirit, and the bride, the church, invite, and say, Come"]}];
